## §1 Variable namespace

### Where state lives

Combat state spans the owner classes below. The owner fixes a field's
scope, lifetime, and valid read/write rules. Use this table to answer
"where does this live and when is it meaningful?".

| Owner | Scope it lives on | Lifetime | Representative variables |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Global catalogue/tuning** | global variable or global variable list | save-persistent; catalogue rebuilt on version mismatch, tuning overrides persist until reset | `POD_combat_moves`, `POD_combat_version`, `global_var:pod_combat_tune_*` |
| **Move definition** | a `POD_combat_move` story on Charon | catalogue initialization/reload → next reload | `skill_key`, `targeting`, `ap_cost`, capability flags, payload and rider fields |
| **Host** | combat host (the PC character) | combat init → combat cleanup | Nothing per-fight lives here. What the host still carries is what CK3 addresses to a character: the `pod_combat.9995` shell-card recipient, character modifiers, character flags, resource costs, the `pod_combat_tier_soak_*` bridge, and the two ATTACKER-side bridges PoD's wound call reads off the wounder (`pod_combat_weapon_attack`, `pod_combat_weapon_soak_pen`). All fight state - lifecycle, counters, identity, outcome, the workspace pair and the target/hit scratch - is on the Fight row |
| **Slot** | a `POD_combat_slot` story (one per combatant, on Charon) | slot creation → combat cleanup | `combat_team`, `team_position`, `slot_char`, `slot_alive`, `slot_morale`, `slot_hit_chance`, stance + hit tier vars |
| **Workspace (current pair)** | the `POD_combat_fight` story | actor/target load → replacement load or combat cleanup; saved after each action without being invalidated | `pod_combat_actor_*`, `pod_combat_target_*`, `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` |
| **Character** | the participant character | field-dependent: PC configuration persists; AI loadout/history and combat flags are per fight | persistent PC `pod_combat_loadout` / `pod_combat_manual_step_pref`; AI `pod_combat_ai_last_move` / `_prev_move` / `_prev2_move`; character-side `pod_combat_tier_soak_*`; character flag `pod_combat_nonfatal_active` |
| **Fight** | a `POD_combat_fight` story on Charon, reached from any participant through `pod_combat_session` | duel launch → combat cleanup or host-death release | `ctx_host`, `ctx_layout`, principals, duel rule flags, follow-up event ID, `pod_combat_active`, `pod_combat_is_over`, `pod_combat_round`, `pod_combat_current_ap`, `pod_combat_current_actor_team`/`_position`, `pod_combat_player_team_size`, `pod_combat_enemy_team_size`, `pod_combat_winner`/`_loser`, `pod_combat_step_mode`, `pod_combat_sc_shell`, `pod_combat_pc_slot`, `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot`, `pod_combat_active_control_slot`, `pod_combat_control_turn_live`, `pod_combat_active_slots`, `pod_combat_log` + `pod_combat_log_seq`, the turn-order track, all AI per-fight scratch, the resolved-action record, `pod_combat_selected_target_*`, `pod_combat_pc_hit_*`, `pod_combat_current_hit_chance`, the workspace pair `pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*`, the find-next scan scratch (`pod_combat_best_*`), `pod_combat_retal_arm_candidate`, `pod_combat_team_all_self_kind`, the reinforcement/summon registries, the mind-control turn flags, and the entire weapon profile. Relocation is complete at layout 9; see **Where state lives, and what stays on a character** |
| **Log entry** | a `POD_combat_log_entry` story (one per row, on Charon) | row push → cap trim or combat cleanup | `entry.var:outcome`, `entry.var:attack_result`, `entry.var:damage`, `entry.var:round`, and the move-context fields the log dispatcher reads |

Two rules are load-bearing across the whole system:

**Authoritative character stats are not stored on the slot.** HP and
max HP come from the PoD wound track. Prowess and soak are sampled from
the character when a workspace side loads; PoD evaluates soak again in
the actual wound call. The slot-scoped wound accessors are:

| SV | Returns |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_slot_max_hp_value` | wound track length (max HP) |
| `pod_combat_slot_total_damage_sv` | total wound boxes filled |
| `pod_combat_slot_agg_damage_sv` | aggravated wound boxes filled |
| `pod_combat_slot_hp_urgency_value` | AI heal urgency derived from the live damaged fraction |

There is **no** `pod_combat_slot_hp_value`. Current HP is computed live
as `max_hp − total_damage`. (A display-only mirror of total/agg/max-HP
exists for GUI perf, refreshed at every HP-change point, but it is never
a logic source.)

**The workspace is a persistent current-pair cache, not durable
per-combatant storage.** `pod_combat_actor_*` /
`pod_combat_target_*` become meaningful when the actor/target pair is
loaded. They remain meaningful after `pod_combat_save_workspace_effect`
and normally persist across that turn; save writes the mirrors back but
does not clear them. A target change or new actor load repoints the
cache. For any non-loaded combatant, read its slot story. Direct slot
writes to the loaded actor or target must also update the workspace or
the next save can overwrite them.

### Where state lives, and what stays on a character

Fight-scoped state lives on the `POD_combat_fight` story, not on the
combat host character. The relocation is **complete** as of layout 9
(`POD_combat_state_layout`, `common/script_values/pod_combat_values.txt`);
the Host row of the table above no longer carries any per-fight state.

The reason was ownership, not tidiness. State on the host is only reachable
while that one character is alive, present, and the player — so the fight
cannot outlive its host's death (a dead character's vars can be read but
not written), cannot be reached by a second player, cannot be driven by a
character who is not seated in it, and cannot be seen by a client whose
own player character is elsewhere. State on a Charon-owned story has none
of those limits.

Three classes of state stay on characters permanently, because the engine
requires it:

- **Anything CK3 addresses to a character:** event recipients such as the
  `pod_combat.9995` shell card (a story cannot receive an event),
  character modifiers, character flags, resource costs, live HP / prowess
  / soak read from PoD, the `pod_combat_tier_soak_*` bridge PoD's
  `POD_soak_chance` reads, and the ATTACKER-side pair PoD's wound call
  reads off the wounder, `pod_combat_weapon_attack` and
  `pod_combat_weapon_soak_pen`. That last name sits four characters from
  the fight-owned `pod_combat_weapon_soak_penalty` and has the opposite
  owner - do not conflate them.
- **Anything that outlives the fight:** `pod_combat_loadout`,
  `pod_combat_unlocked_moves`, `pod_combat_saved_loadout_*`,
  `pod_combat_manual_step_pref`, `pod_combat_config_open`, and the
  move-sheet / filter vars.
- **Per-character AI memory:** `pod_combat_ai_last_move` / `_prev_move` /
  `_prev2_move`, the focus trio, `_target_pref` and the `_pers_*`
  personality vars. They describe that AI, not that fight, so they ride
  the character even though they are cleared per fight.

`pod_combat_session` is a character var too, but it is the pointer, not
the state. `ctx_host` names the character a fight addresses its events to,
which is a different question from where the fight's state lives. The move
story keeps four of its own that never moved: `pod_combat_ai_score`,
`pod_combat_ai_admitted`, `pod_combat_ai_bonus_met` and
`pod_combat_ai_eff_ap_drain`.

The fight stamps `ctx_layout` from `POD_combat_state_layout` (currently
**9**). A fight whose stamp does not match the running code was built by a
different layout, so its vars would be read from the wrong owner; it is
torn down rather than resumed. Mid-fight saves do not survive a layout
change.

**`?=` behaves differently in triggers than in effects.** In an EFFECT,
`var:X ?= { ... }` skips the block when `X` is unset. In a TRIGGER it does
**not**: the link resolves to an empty scope and the inner trigger evaluates
against it anyway, producing three errors per call (`Failed to fetch variable`,
`Event target link 'var' returned an unset scope`, `has_variable trigger [ This
scope doesn't support variables. Scope: empty ]`). A gate that can be evaluated
with no fight running therefore needs presence tested FIRST, and nested, because
a sibling does not short-circuit either:

```
some_trigger = {
	trigger_if = {
		limit = { has_variable = pod_combat_session }
		var:pod_combat_session ?= { has_variable = pod_combat_active }
	}
	trigger_else = { always = no }
}
```

`has_variable` on a character never errors, so it is safe as the outer test; the
inner `?=` still earns its place by covering a pointer left at an ended story.
This cost ~1100 error-log lines in one session from `pod_combat_is_active_trigger`
alone, because the loadout decision's `is_valid` evaluates it constantly out of
combat.

**Reaching the fight: never deref `pod_combat_session` bare.** Use
`var:pod_combat_session ?= { ... }`, or gate the block on
`has_variable = pod_combat_session`. This holds even inside an effect that
only ever runs mid-fight, because the tooltip builder walks the
duel-launch chain symbolically *before* the fight exists — it skips
`create_story` (see **`create_story` is skipped by the tooltip builder**
above), so the pointer is legitimately unset there. A bare deref errors on
every duel confirm-tooltip. `verify_fight_scope_guards.py` enforces this.

**Wrapping a write relocates its value expression too.** Putting an
existing `set_variable` inside `var:pod_combat_session ?= { ... }` moves
not just the write target but the whole `value = ...` expression into the
fight scope. A host-scope script value evaluated there does not error - it
silently returns **0**. So a mechanical "wrap the write" refactor turns a
correct number into a zero with no log line. Either hoist the value first
(`save_scope_value_as` outside the block, then `value = scope:x` inside -
safe for numbers, never for flags, see **Scope traps inside slot loops**),
or re-root the expression as an absolute chain
(`scope:slot_story.var:X`, `scope:combat_host.var:Y`) that resolves
identically from either scope. The load/save helpers use the second form
throughout, which is what kept a 30-field relocation to one line per
field.

**Inside a fight-scope block, never start a NEW `pod_combat_session`
deref.** `this` is already the fight story, and a story has no
`pod_combat_session` of its own - the pointer is a *character* var. A
`var:pod_combat_session ?= { ... }` nested inside another one therefore
asks the fight for its own pointer and always resolves unset: it skips the
block in an effect, and errors in a trigger. Reach back out through
`scope:combat_host` instead.

### Variable reference

Lookup tables, grouped by category. Names verified against
`pod_combat_values.txt` and `pod_combat_group_effects.txt`.

#### Core fight state (on the `POD_combat_fight` story)

Reached from a participant as `var:pod_combat_session ?= { ... }` in an
EFFECT. In a TRIGGER that guard does not skip an unset pointer - test
`has_variable = pod_combat_session` first, and nested (see **`?=` behaves
differently in triggers than in effects**).

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_active` | flag: combat is in progress |
| `pod_combat_is_over` | flag: combat is over (re-entry guard) |
| `pod_combat_round` | current round number |
| `pod_combat_current_ap` | AP remaining this turn |
| `pod_combat_current_actor_team` / `_position` | the acting combatant's team flag + position (0 = leader) |
| `pod_combat_player_team_size` | count of seated player positions; incremented as each ally is added |
| `pod_combat_enemy_team_size` | count of seated enemy positions; incremented as each enemy is added |
| `pod_combat_selected_target_team` / `_position` | the slot currently loaded into the target workspace |
| `pod_combat_ai_enemy_finishable` | a finishable enemy exists; the OR of the two per-kind vars below |
| `pod_combat_ai_enemy_finishable_hp` / `_mor` | per damage kind - each also requires the actor to hold an affordable move of that kind |
| `pod_combat_ai_cur_target_finishable` | the CURRENT target is itself finishable; the kill-snap's gate |
| `pod_combat_ai_can_dmg_hp` / `_mor` | the actor can deal that kind of damage this turn; gates the kill-priority arms |
| `pod_combat_pc_slot` | pointer to the player/0 (PC) slot story |
| `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot` | pointer to the enemy/0 (primary opponent) slot story |
| `pod_combat_active_control_slot` | pointer to the slot the human is driving. Re-pointed at every hand-off and never cleared mid-fight, so the target readout and TARGETED badges survive the AI phase |
| `pod_combat_control_turn_live` | presence: a controlled turn is handed off and uncommitted. The O(1) "is it my turn" latch every per-frame GUI gate reads |
| `pod_combat_active_slots` | variable list of every filled slot story; iterated by every dispatch loop |
| `pod_combat_summoned_chars` | variable list of mid-fight summons, disappeared at combat end |

`pod_combat_pc_slot` and `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot` are the **only**
literal slot pointers. Any non-leader slot is reached by iterating
`pod_combat_active_slots` and matching `combat_team` + `team_position`.
`pod_combat_nonfatal_active` is not host state: it is a character flag
stamped on every participant of every fight, whatever the fatality mode,
so PoD's inline wound-death check cannot fire mid-action. Stripped at
combat end; the fatal modes' deaths are then settled in one pass at the
writeback (see **Combat end** and **Deferred death resolution**).

#### Per-slot stats (on each `POD_combat_slot` story)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `combat_team` | flag (`flag:player` / `flag:enemy`): the slot's team, baked at creation |
| `team_position` | int 0+: position within the team (0 = leader), baked at creation |
| `slot_char` | character ref occupying the slot |
| `slot_alive` | 1 while in play, 0 when KO'd |
| `slot_morale` | current Morale |
| `slot_hit_chance` | live hit chance (20–95) |
| `slot_base_hit_chance` | hit chance before any tier effects |
| `slot_initiative` | initiative roll this round |
| `slot_init_bonus` | next-round initiative modifier |
| `slot_ap_drain` | pending AP drain, consumed after the victim's next-turn AP budget is seeded; subtracts from that budget and floors at 0 |
| `slot_energy_used` | counter feeding AI energy-attrition weighting |
| `slot_turn_order` | turn rank within the round (1 = first) |
| `slot_has_acted` | 1 once the slot has acted this round; reset each round start |
| `slot_dot_amount` / `slot_dot_rounds` | active burn: damage per round-end tick / rounds remaining |
| `slot_dot_kind` | literal flag typing the burn: `flag:morale`, `flag:aggravated`, an elemental class, or `flag:duel` (superficial) |
| `slot_dot_source` | character ref of the burn's caster; the round-end tick credits it as the wound attacker if still alive, else self-attributes the tick to the victim |
| `slot_controlled` | mind-control marker: the slot's next AI turn serves the other side |
| `slot_human_controller` | character ref: this slot is driven by that human, not by the AI. Presence is what every turn-control gate tests. Do not confuse it with `slot_controlled` two rows up, which means the opposite |
| `slot_target_team` / `_position` | this slot's standing target: team + position. Survives its own AI phase; auto-retargets when the target is KO'd |
| `slot_target_char` | character ref of that target, read by the top-row "Target: X" display |

#### Who controls a turn

Board position is not control. A slot is human-driven iff it carries
`slot_human_controller`; today exactly one slot is marked (the PC's, at
finalize), so behaviour is identical to the old `player`/`0` literal test that
was inlined at nine engine sites. Marking more slots is what clones, proxy
fights and multiplayer seats each need, and none of them has to touch the turn
engine to get it.

Two tiers read the marker, and mixing them up is a performance bug:

- **Engine tier** - `pod_combat_current_actor_is_controlled_trigger` walks
  `pod_combat_active_slots` for the slot matching the current-actor pointers and
  tests the marker. Correct anywhere, but it is a roster walk: use it only at
  turn-advance decision points (finalize, `advance_one_ai_turn`, the pre-PC loop,
  the two settle effects, `group_end_round`, the CK3 resource gate).
- **GUI tier** - `pod_combat_is_player_turn_trigger` and
  `pod_combat_can_advance_trigger` read the `pod_combat_control_turn_live` latch
  instead, and `pod_combat_advance_pending_value` is its script-value form for
  the End Turn / Advance swap. These sit on the documented hotspot (four
  `IsValid` evaluations per move button per frame), so they must stay O(1).

The latch is raised at the head of `pod_combat_setup_pc_turn_effect` (which also
re-points `pod_combat_active_control_slot`) and dropped at the PC commit in
`pod_combat_process_group_turns_effect`, with a belt clear at the head of
`pod_combat_execute_ai_turn_effect`. It is deliberately narrower than the
pointer: the pointer must survive the AI phase for the display, the latch must
not.

#### Per-slot stance flags (on the slot story)

All persist across rounds; cleared only when consumed or at combat end.

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `slot_is_guarding` | Guard stance active |
| `slot_is_dodging` | Dodge stance active |
| `slot_is_stealthed` | Stealth active (adds `pod_combat_stealth_hit_bonus`, default +30, to hit chance) |
| `slot_retaliate_hit` / `slot_retaliate_miss` / `slot_retaliate_any` | retaliation damage if hit / if missed / on any resolved attack |
| `slot_retaliate_{hit,miss,any}_kind` | flag typing the counter: `flag:morale`, `flag:aggravated`, or an elemental class; unset = plain |
| `slot_retaliate_{hit,miss,any}_kind_from_weapon` | presence companion marking the kind as WEAPON-derived rather than authored - the fire-time waste guard reads it |
| `slot_retaliate_{hit,miss,any}_morale` | presence companion to a `morale` kind (drives the slot-card status tooltip) |

#### Per-slot spirit-damage capability (on the slot story)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `slot_incorporeal_spirit` | combatant is incorporeal; physical attacks no-op unless the attacker can damage spirits |
| `slot_can_damage_spirit` | combatant can land physical hits on incorporeal targets |

Both are write-once facts set at slot init from static
trait/perk/artifact triggers; they never change mid-fight.

#### Tier variables

Tier = 1 (small, ±10), 2 (medium, ±20), 3 (large, ±30). See **Tier system**.

Hit tiers live on the slot story; live hit chance is rebuilt from base plus the
sum of active tier magnitudes:

```
slot_hit_{direction}_{duration}_tier
  direction: buff | debuff
  duration:  round (cleared at the holder's turn boundary) | combat (lasts the fight)
```

Soak tiers live on the COMBATANT, not the slot, as
`pod_combat_tier_soak_{direction}_{duration}`. They are read directly by
`POD_soak_chance`, so the tier a card shows is the tier the wound roll rolls.

The eight magnitude script values
(`pod_combat_slot_{stat}_{direction}_{duration}_mag_sv`) return the
matching tier's magnitude (small/medium/large) or 0 - the four hit
variants read the slot vars, the four soak variants read the occupant's
bridge vars.
`pod_combat_slot_hit_net_mod_value` sums the four hit mags signed (buffs
− debuffs) for the NPC slot-card display.

#### Workspace mirror (transient, on the fight story)

The workspace mirrors the slot fields for whoever is acting
(`pod_combat_actor_*`) and whoever is targeted (`pod_combat_target_*`).

| Variable | Mirrors |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*` | the matching `slot_*` field on the acting / targeted slot (morale, hit_chance, char, energy_used, stance flags, init_bonus, ap_drain, all retaliation vars, the 4 hit tier vars, both spirit flags) |
| `pod_combat_actor_soak` / `_prowess` (and target twins) | NOT mirrors: live values stashed off the combatant (see below) |
| `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` | the active attacker's hit chance for this attack |

Both sides load and save through one symmetric pair,
`pod_combat_load_side_from_story_helper` /
`pod_combat_save_side_to_story_helper` (`SIDE = actor | target`). The
actor and both save wrappers are thin delegates.
`pod_combat_load_target_from_story_effect` additionally recalculates
both sides' hit chance for the newly loaded actor-target pair. **To add
a mirrored field:** one line in each helper (load + save), and nothing
else. The workspace is fight-owned, so the whole pair is dropped
atomically by the `end_story` that ends the fight. Do **not** add a
`remove_variable` to `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` or to
`pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect` - both removal lists are gone, and a
re-added line would be dead code that only invites someone to "fix" it
into a real bug later. The same holds for the load-only presence flags
(the spirit mirrors), which used to need a `group_cleanup` removal.

**Soak and prowess are not mirrored fields.** `pod_combat_stash_side_live_stats_effect`
(called at the tail of the load helper, and again after a move writes a soak
tier) reads `POD_soak_chance` and `prowess` off the side's character and stores
them as `pod_combat_$SIDE$_soak` / `_prowess`. They are per-action scratch, never
written back to the slot. The stash exists purely to hoist the read: the AI
weighting reads both inside `random_list` modifiers, i.e. once per candidate
move, and `POD_soak_chance` is far too heavy to re-walk that often.

**Two fields are load-only:** `slot_can_damage_spirit` and
`slot_incorporeal_spirit` are copied into the workspace on load but
never written back on save. They are static facts that never mutate
mid-fight, so the slot copy cannot diverge. A move that changed spirit
capability mid-fight would need both a save-helper writeback and a
slot-story writer.

Two workspace-scoped accessor SVs read off the actor's character
directly: `pod_combat_actor_hp_value` (empty wound boxes = HP remaining)
and `pod_combat_actor_max_hp_value` (track length). They gate on
`pod_combat_actor_char` being set and alive.

#### Result / display (on the fight story)

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_winner` / `pod_combat_loser` | character ref: who won / lost |
| `pod_combat_last_damage` | last HP damage dealt |
| `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` | last HP heal applied |
| `pod_combat_last_morale_damage_amount` / `_last_morale_heal_amount` | last morale damage / heal |
| `pod_combat_last_self_damage` | post-soak recoil damage applied to the actor |
| `pod_combat_last_energy_gain` / `_last_energy_drain` | requested conditional energy bonus amount when that bonus fired; the splat helpers do not export the pool-capped applied delta |
| `pod_combat_last_ap_refund` | AP given back by a fired `ap_refund` bonus (§14). Exists so the log can render the refund only when the condition actually held, rather than off the move's declared `bonus_effect` |
| `pod_combat_last_ap_drain` | effective AP drain applied to the target mirror at §14: the base `ap_drain` plus a fired `ap_drain` bonus. The log reads this rather than the move's base var, so a bonus-only drain renders at all and a base+bonus stack can read as a full stun. Only written when the total is above 0, and only inside §14's control-failure guard |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_damage` | last retaliation damage |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_agg` / `_last_retal_morale` | actual-result markers for the counter: it landed aggravated (PoD's per-victim classification) / it eroded morale instead of HP |
| `pod_combat_last_result_agg` | actual-result marker for the main damage line: PoD classified the landed wound as aggravated for this victim (lifted from the route helper's `scope:result_agg`) — see **Result model** |
| `pod_combat_last_soaked_count` | points absorbed by PoD's per-point soak roll on the last damage write (lifted from the route helper's `scope:soaked_count`); absent = none. Drives the damage line's partial-soak note — see **Result model** |
| `pod_combat_last_outcome` | optional top-level fight outcome flag (`flag:attack` / `heal` / `morale_damage` / `morale_healed`); a pure utility move leaves it absent and `push_log` creates `entry.var:outcome = flag:utility` — see **Result model** |
| `pod_combat_last_attack_result` | inner attack resolution (`flag:hp_damage` / `soaked` / `immune` / `missed` / `dodged` / `stealthed` / `guard_blocked`); only meaningful when outcome = attack |
| `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` | overlay flag: a retaliation fired on this row |
| `pod_combat_aoe_any_hp_damage` | AoE latch: any target took HP damage this burst |

One transient marker lives on the acting **character**, not the fight
story:
`pod_combat_force_agg`, set and removed around a single elemental
dispatch when the `change_type` bonus must force PoD's classifier to
aggravated. The `POD_damage_type_is_aggravated` delta reads it through
`POD_wound_attacker_forces_agg_trigger`, which answers no when no
`scope:wound_attacker` exists. See **Damage pipeline**.

#### AI move-history (on the acting AI character, not the fight story)

These are **story references** pointing at a `POD_combat_move` scope,
written directly on the acting AI character after each pick. They are
per-fight scratch, cleared at AI slot initialization and again at
combat cleanup alongside that AI character's `pod_combat_loadout`. The
AI's anti-repetition penalties compare them by scope-equality.

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `pod_combat_ai_last_move` | most recent move |
| `pod_combat_ai_prev_move` | the move 2 turns ago |
| `pod_combat_ai_prev2_move` | the move 3 turns ago |

They form a 3-deep ring, shifted on each pick: `prev2 ← prev`, `prev ←
last`, `last ← chosen`.

