## §2 Execution phases - `POD_execute_combat_move`

Every combat move runs through one generic effect:
**`POD_execute_combat_move`** (in `pod_combat_move_effects.txt`). There
is no per-move code. The effect reads the move's attributes off its
`POD_combat_move` story_cycle (passed in as `$STORY$`) and applies
whatever it finds — damage, heals, stance flags, tier buffs, reactions,
summons. A move *is* the set of variables on its story; execution is the
engine that interprets them.

**Caller scope is the combat host** (the PC - not because the state is
there, but because every fight-scope deref in the move pipeline starts
from `scope:combat_host.var:pod_combat_session`). Before the call, the
caller loads the workspace alias pair (`pod_combat_actor_*` /
`pod_combat_target_*`) onto the **fight story** for whoever is acting this
turn:

- **actor** = whoever is taking the action (the PC, or any AI combatant)
- **target** = the current single target (rotates per AoE iteration /
  per AI target-swap)

Two callers drive it: the `pod_combat_execute_move` scripted GUI (PC
button) and `pod_combat_ai_select_and_execute_move_effect` (AI turn).

### Phases

The body is a flat, top-to-bottom sequence. Most payload phases are
guarded by a move-story field, while setup/reset (§0/§2), the
result-aware log gate (§16), and teardown (§17) are engine phases with
their own contracts. The ordering is load-bearing — see **Phase
ordering rules** below before moving anything.

| Phase | What it does |
|---|---|
| §0 | Save the host as `scope:combat_host`. Long-lived; AoE iteration bodies re-enter host scope through it without their own root-save. |
| §1 | Spend `ap_cost` AP (`pod_combat_spend_ap_effect`). |
| §1b | If `once_per` is present, append this move story to the actor slot's round- or combat-duration used list. The usability gate rejects later attempts; the round list clears during initiative setup. |
| §2 | Reset the result axes (`pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect`), set `pod_combat_last_damage = 0`, and clear the pure-control delivery-failure latch. Absence of a `pod_combat_last_*` var now means "no outcome yet" for this move. |
| §2b | **Bonus condition pre-evaluation.** For a `cond_mode = bonus` move whose condition holds (`pod_combat_move_condition_met_trigger`), set the fight flag `pod_combat_bonus_active` and stamp the §6 fight vars (`pod_combat_bonus_damage` / `pod_combat_bonus_aggravated`). Each later phase then grants its bonus iff the flag is set and `bonus_effect` matches: §5d `hit`, §6 `damage`/`change_type`/`change_class`, §7 `morale_damage` (folded into the base deal call), §9b `self_heal`/`heal_target`/`self_morale_heal`/`energy_gain`/`energy_drain`, §12 `stance_*`, §13 `retaliate_boost` (folded into the charge the move arms), §13b-bonus `dot`, §13b-bonus-morale `morale_dot`, §14 `ap_refund` / `ap_drain` (both stamp a transient - `pod_combat_last_ap_refund`, `pod_combat_last_ap_drain` - so the log renders what actually fired; the drain lift carries base + bonus summed). (Gate mode is enforced pre-execution in the usability triggers, not here.) `uses_weapon` is the one bonus with no phase branch of its own: it grants the weapon swing instead, which §2c and §6 pick up through `pod_combat_move_swings_weapon_trigger` - so §2b running before §2c is load-bearing for it. `change_class` is the only bonus with a second CSV column of its own (`bonus_param`, the element it converts to); §6 switches on that move-story var in place rather than copying it to a fight var, which would degrade the flag. |
| §2c | **Weapon profile.** For a move that swings the actor's weapon - the static `uses_weapon` flag, or the `uses_weapon` bonus whose condition held at §2b, both answered by `pod_combat_move_swings_weapon_trigger` - resolve the actor's equipped `primary_armament` (subject to `can_benefit_from_artifact`) into six per-action transients on the **fight story**: `pod_combat_weapon_class` (the retype), `pod_combat_weapon_bonus_damage`, `pod_combat_weapon_undodgeable`, `pod_combat_weapon_heal_on_damage`, `pod_combat_weapon_energy_on_damage`, `pod_combat_weapon_soak_penalty` (the last three consumed in the §9c recovery riders and the dispatch-site shred stamp; note that the fight-owned `pod_combat_weapon_soak_penalty` is a DIFFERENT variable from the character-side `pod_combat_weapon_soak_pen` the §6 dispatch site copies onto the attacker for PoD's wound call). Unconditional set-or-clear, so an unarmed actor or a non-weapon move actively clears the previous action's profile. The retype is only ever seeded over a move whose own class is `superficial` - an authored element or `aggravated` is the move's identity and outranks the weapon. Torn down in §17 and, as insurance, in `pod_combat_cleanup_effect`. See **Weapon artifact overrides**. |
| §9c | **Weapon recovery riders.** `artifact_weapon_heal_on_damage` / `_energy_on_damage`, gated on `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_hp_damage_trigger` - the same predicate the `on_hp_damage` rider resolves to, which reads the live result pair on a single target and the `pod_combat_aoe_any_hp_damage` latch on a sweep. So recovery fires **once per action, not once per victim**, and a fully-soaked blow (`flag:soaked`) feeds nothing. Reuses `pod_combat_restore_hp_superficial_effect` and `POD_splat_agnostic_energy_gain` (actor scope-switch mandatory), and shares `pod_combat_last_energy_gain` with the §9b bonus - adding to it rather than overwriting, so a move carrying both logs the sum. Sits before §15, so the actor cannot have been killed by a counter and needs no `is_alive` guard. Inherited quirks: the heal lift fires the §16 AoE wrap-up row even at 0, and on a sweep stamps a heal outcome on that row (both pre-existing §9b behaviours); the energy line logs the requested amount, not the pool-capped delta. |
| §3 | Pay `energy_cost` from the actor's splat pool. Scope-switches to `var:pod_combat_actor_char` first (`POD_splat_agnostic_energy_cost` drains the *current* scope's pool — without the switch every AI cast would drain the PC). Also bumps the AI energy-attrition counter. |
| §4 | Pay CK3 resources (`gold_cost` / `stress_cost` / `piety_cost` / `prestige_cost`) and apply `steal_gold` / `gain_dread`. **Controlled turns only** — gated on `pod_combat_current_actor_is_controlled_trigger`; AI turns are free. The payer splits by primitive: gold / piety / prestige charge the driver (the affordability gate reads that same purse), while stress and the `steal_gold` / `gain_dread` gains land on the acting body. Practice still pays declared costs but suppresses gold theft and Dread gain. |
| §5 | Strip the single target's stances **before** §6 so the attack resolves against the stripped state: `bypass_dodge` removes the dodge flag; `bypass_stealth` runs the workspace stealth-break (also removes the current stealth hit bonus); ordinary `remove_guard` clears the target's guard so a strip-guard + attack lands its own hit. A `requires_control_contact` guard strip waits for §5b2 instead. |
| §5b | `team_enemy` / `team_all`: broadcast tier debuffs and stance-strips to **all** swept slots before the damage loop, so each per-target hit respects them (`pod_combat_apply_team_enemy_debuffs_effect` / `pod_combat_apply_team_all_debuffs_effect`). `team_all` additionally applies the caster's own tier share actor-side (`pod_combat_apply_team_all_self_debuffs_effect`). |
| §5b2 | **Pure-control contact delivery.** An explicitly typed single-enemy move with no HP/morale payload carries `requires_control_contact`; run the unified capability resolver here. On failure, set a latch that suppresses its guard strip, tiers, mind control, AP drain, and initiative debuff. AP/energy/once-per are still spent, actor stealth is consumed, and the log keeps only the miss/block outcome rather than claiming payloads landed. |
| §5c | **Tier buffs/debuffs.** Buffs → actor except `single_ally` → selected target; debuffs → target. Each write is **upgrade-only** (first application or a strictly higher tier), and a failed §5b2 delivery skips all declared tiers. Hit/soak recalc and the PoD soak bridge follow the actual beneficiary. See **Tier system**. |
| §5d | For a fired `bonus_effect = hit`, add `bonus_value` to `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` for this move and re-cap it at `pod_combat_hit_chance_max`. §17 rebuilds the ordinary tier+stealth value afterward so the bonus cannot leak to a later move. |
| §6 | **HP damage dispatch.** Routes `single_enemy` vs the `team_enemy` / `team_all` AoE sweeps. Resolves the hit and writes the result axes `pod_combat_last_outcome = flag:attack` + `pod_combat_last_attack_result` on the fight story. See **Damage pipeline**. |
| §7 | Morale damage (`pod_combat_deal_morale_damage_effect`, or a team variant). Own pipeline: auto-hit, no guard, no soak, but stealth/dodge still block. Rider-gated by `morale_damage_condition`. A held morale bonus folds into the same deal call as the base amount (one stance consumption, combined total logged), including the `team_enemy` / `team_all` broadcasts. |
| §8 | Self-damage to the actor (`pod_combat_deal_hp_damage_bypass_guard_effect`, `REASON = duel`): generic duel-class wound damage, normally superficial and soakable, while PoD retains authority to promote/classify it and decide aggravated soakability. Rider-gated by `self_damage_condition`. Lifts the post-soak amount into `pod_combat_last_self_damage`; the attack's result axes are untouched (recoil never overwrites the main outcome). |
| §9 | HP heal. `heal_type = aggravated` heals aggravated boxes too; else superficial (caps at `max_hp − agg_damage`). Routes to actor, `single_ally`, `single_pc`, or `team_ally`. There is deliberately no `team_all` arm (it would mend the enemies the same move just hit); such a move heals only its caster. Rider-gated by `heal_condition`; a drain heal must explicitly set `heal_condition = on_hp_damage`, as the current drain moves do. Must run **after** §6 so the rider reads the right outcome. |
| §9b | Conditional bonus delivery: `self_heal` restores superficial HP to the actor, `heal_target` to the target, `self_morale_heal` restores the actor's morale (actor only - the unconditional `morale_heal` column already covers allies), `energy_gain` restores the actor's splat pool, and `energy_drain` drains the target outside practice. These are independent of the base §9 heal and lift fired energy amounts for the log; the morale heal needs no log wiring of its own because the primitive already writes `pod_combat_last_morale_heal_amount`. |
| §10 | Morale heal (`pod_combat_restore_morale_effect`). Routes to actor, `single_ally`, `single_pc`, or `team_ally`. No rider condition. |
| §12 | Non-tier stance flags: `set_guard`, `dodge`, `stealth` (idempotent). They apply to the selected target for `single_ally`, otherwise to the actor. (`remove_guard` is a hostile target strip in §5/§5b2.) |
| §12b | `team_ally` only: broadcast the move-declared §5c/§12 tiers and stance flags, plus the soak bridge var, to all ally slots (`pod_combat_apply_team_ally_buffs_effect`). A condition-granted `stance_*` bonus is actor-only and is not part of this broadcast. |
| §12c | `marker_modifier`: add the named CK3 character modifier to the actor (`add_character_modifier`, `days = 30`). A literal switch on the move's `marker_modifier` flag, because `add_character_modifier` needs a literal key. The marker carries no numeric effects; it exists so a **later** move's `has_modifier` condition can read it. Runs after §2b, so a move can never satisfy its own `has_modifier` condition, and the payoff is always cross-move. See §11 **Adding a marker character modifier**. |
| §13 | Arm retaliation stances on the actor for a later eligible §15 attack: `retaliate_if_hit` (counters on contact — thorns) / `retaliate_if_missed` (counters on evasion — riposte) / `retaliate_if_attacked` (counters any resolved attack — always). Amount falls back `retaliate_damage → hp_damage → 1`, staged once into the fight temp `pod_combat_retal_arm_candidate`, then increased by a fired `retaliate_boost` bonus. Because the take-strongest test and the write both read that staged number, a boosted charge cannot lose a comparison it should win; a move arming several families boosts each. Arming is take-strongest **within one charge family**: only a strictly larger amount replaces that family's current reaction, and equal amounts never replace its kind. Kind precedence is morale > `retaliate_agg` > the move's elemental class > a granted `change_class` element > the equipped weapon's retype > unset (plain); a transient-source kind (weapon retype or conversion, both marked with the historically-named `_kind_from_weapon` var) stands down at fire time if the eventual victim is immune to it (`pod_combat_stamped_type_is_wasted_trigger`). |
| §13b | Damage-over-time: seed `slot_dot_amount/rounds/kind/source` on the target's slot (single-target). `slot_dot_source` records the caster (`pod_combat_actor_char`) so the round-end tick credits the right killer. Delivery excludes only a miss-class result and a target already marked KO: dodged / stealthed / missed does not seed, but `guard_blocked`, full `soaked`, and `immune` are not miss codes and currently do seed; a landed hp0 morale DoT with no `attack_result` also applies. Kind is seeded as literal flags (default `duel`, the move's damage class, morale wins); re-apply refreshes. Pushes the "is afflicted" row with its per-round amount and HP/Morale track. HP ticks bypass per-point soak but retain immunity, Clay Man protection, weakness multiplier/cap, and PoD wound classification; the application row does not predict that future victim-specific class. See **Combat log** / **Turn loop**. |
| §13b-bonus | A fired `bonus_effect = dot` seeds the same target-slot DoT using `bonus_value` as its per-tick amount and `pod_combat_bonus_dot_rounds` (fixed at 2) as its duration. It uses the same miss/KO delivery gate and damage-class kind seeding as §13b. |
| §13b-bonus-morale | A fired `bonus_effect = morale_dot` seeds the same slot with the kind forced to `morale`, so it erodes will rather than flesh, and pushes the morale affliction row (`dot_applied_morale`) instead of the generic one. The force lives inside `pod_combat_seed_dot_on_slot_effect` as a `$STORY$` read ordered after the weapon retype, exactly like the static `dot_morale` flag - a post-call slot write from here would hit the iteration-scope trap that effect's contract warns about. The generator rejects `morale_dot` on a move that also has a base DoT, since both write the one `slot_dot_*` slot and the second would silently erase the first. |
| §13c | Mind control (`mind_control` flag): mark the target slot `slot_controlled` (steals one turn). Players are immune; `POD_has_strong_mental_defenses_trigger` blocks unless the caster passes `can_bypass_mental_defenses_trigger`. A pure control requires successful §5b2 delivery; a morale-bearing control additionally requires proof that morale damage landed, so dodge/stealth never still seizes the target. Blocked casts post a resist row. Single-target only. |
| §14 | Parametric flags: `ap_gain` and a fired `ap_refund` bonus add to `pod_combat_current_ap`; the refund also stamps the transient fight var `pod_combat_last_ap_refund` (cleared in `pod_combat_clear_per_target_state_effect`, mirrored onto the log entry as `ap_refund`, and part of the §16 wrap-up gate below) so the ApRefund log segment renders only when the bonus actually fired; base + conditional `ap_drain` are summed, take-max into the target mirror, and consumed at the target's next turn start, stamping the effective total as `pod_combat_last_ap_drain` (same lifecycle as the refund lift) so the ApDrain segment renders a bonus-only drain and labels a base+bonus stack correctly; `initiative_debuff` stacks negatively into `pod_combat_target_init_bonus` for the next initiative fold. Target-side drain/debuff is suppressed by a failed §5b2 delivery. |
| §14b | `summons_combatant`: run the move's character-creation recipe, then seat the result on the resolved friendly or `summon_hostile` destination (`pod_combat_summon_combatant_glue_effect`). After the damage phases (a same-cast AoE can't hit the new unit), before §15 (it can't counter on its summon turn); it acts next round. |
| §15 | **Reaction phase.** Only a move carrying `hp_damage` whose targeting is neither `team_enemy` nor `team_all` checks the target's armed retaliation (`pod_combat_check_retaliation_effect`). Pure morale/control and team AoE moves do not trigger reactions. At most one counter fires: a specific hit/miss charge answers first and holds the always-charge. See **Reactions**. |
| §16 | Push the combat-log row (`pod_combat_push_log_effect`). AoE-aware gate, below. |
| §17 | Bonus teardown: if §5d temporarily changed hit chance, rebuild the actor's ordinary tier+stealth value first; then clear `pod_combat_bonus_active` / `pod_combat_bonus_damage` / `pod_combat_bonus_aggravated`. Also belt-and-braces-removes the actor's transient `pod_combat_force_agg` marker (normally dropped at the dispatch site) for any future early-exit path. |

### The §16 log gate

Non-AoE moves always push their one summary row. AoE moves are
different: the AoE iterators in §6/§7/§9/§10 push **one row per
target** as they go and set the `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows`
tripwire. When that tripwire is set, §16 normally skips its wrap-up row
— the per-target rows already are the log entry.

§16 fires anyway when actor-side state landed that the per-target rows
could not carry. The gate is an `OR` that fires the push when **any** of
these hold:

- `pod_combat_aoe_phase_pushed_rows` is **not** set (non-AoE path —
  always push), or
- `pod_combat_last_heal_amount` is set (actor self-heal), or
- `pod_combat_last_morale_heal_amount` is set (actor self-morale-heal),
  or
- `pod_combat_last_retal_fired` is set (a counter resolved), or
- `pod_combat_last_self_damage` is set (recoil is applied once to the
  actor, not per AoE target, so the per-target rows never carry it), or
- `pod_combat_last_energy_gain` / `pod_combat_last_energy_drain` is set
  (energy bonuses apply once at §9b, after the per-target loop), or
- `pod_combat_last_ap_refund` is set (the refund is credited once at
  §14, after the per-target pushes, so without this arm an AoE move's
  refund would never get a row), or
- `pod_combat_last_ap_drain` is set (§14 applies the drain at the same
  point; inert while every drain move is single-enemy, since those never
  set the tripwire in the first place).

### Phase ordering rules

These orderings are required, not stylistic:

- **§5 / §5b / §5c before §6.** Damage must resolve against stripped
  stances and applied tiers, so a strip / buff / debuff + attack lands on
  its own hit. Reversed, hits land on un-stripped dodge/stealth/guard, a
  pre-buff hit chance, and un-debuffed soak.
- **§5b2 before §5c/§12/§13c/§14.** Pure-control delivery must resolve
  before any target-side payload can write; the failure latch is the shared
  gate that prevents an evaded control from applying only part of its effects.
- **§7 / §8 / §9 after §6.** Their rider gates read
  `pod_combat_last_outcome` and `pod_combat_last_attack_result`, both
  written by §6. Reordering breaks the `on_hp_damage` / `on_contact` /
  `on_miss` conditions.
- **§15 after §6.** Retaliation gates on §6's outcome; running it
  earlier reads stale state from the previous move.
- **§16 after all outcome-producing phases and before §17 teardown.**
  The log row captures every result axis and amount field. Pushed
  earlier, it records a partial result and misses the rider amounts
  later phases populate.
- **§14b (summon) after the damage/AoE phases and before §15.** Keeps a
  same-cast AoE off the new unit and stops it countering on its summon
  turn.

### Damage pipeline

HP damage is the one place combat hands work to PoD. The pipeline
decides **whether** the blow connects (hit roll, guard, dodge/stealth,
spirit gate), then routes the connecting blow into PoD's wound system,
which owns **how much** lands (soak, splat weakness, aggravated
classification, the death check). Combat never touches wound boxes
directly.

#### Capability tags decide the pipeline shape

Each move carries up to four capability flags on its story, authored
directly in its registry setter block. The contact resolver
(`pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect`) reads them off
`scope:current_move` and walks the same decision tree for every move,
branching only where a flag is present:

| Capability flag | Effect when present |
|---|---|
| `checks_spirit_gate` | A physical attack passes harmlessly through an incorporeal spirit target unless the actor has the spirit-damage capability. |
| `requires_hit_roll` | Roll `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` vs 100. Absent = auto-hit. |
| `checks_guard` | Consume the target's guard and block the blow. Absent = guard does nothing (this is how a move "bypasses guard"). |
| `checks_stance` | Run stance consumption: stealth then dodge auto-miss the blow. |

The common physical profile sets all four flags. The common
supernatural profile sets only `checks_stance`: it auto-hits, ignores
guard, reaches spirits, but can still be evaded by dodge or stealth.
These are authoring conventions, not a runtime `attack_type` field.
Morale damage has its own auto-hit/no-guard/no-soak path and consumes
the target's stealth/dodge regardless of these capability flags, so
"mental" is not a contact-resolver capability profile.

For a `single_enemy` move with zero HP and morale damage, authors set
`requires_control_contact` explicitly when its control payload must
invoke this resolver before being applied. The flag does not change the
resolver's shape; it connects the otherwise payload-free move to it.
Omitting the flag keeps pure utility/control guaranteed.

#### Contact resolution

`pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect` walks the tree and writes
`scope:contact_result` (a resolver-local integer, distinct from the
result-axis flags):

1. **Spirit gate** — if the move checks it, the target is an incorporeal
   spirit, and the actor can't damage spirits → result `6`, logged as
   `missed`.
2. **Stance consume** — if `checks_stance`, run
   `pod_combat_consume_target_stance_effect`: stealth first (result `4`,
   logged `stealthed`), then dodge (result `3`, logged `dodged`).
   Stealth is consumed before dodge so a stealthed-and-dodging target
   keeps its dodge for the next attack.
3. **Hit roll** — if `requires_hit_roll`, a `random_list` weighted by
   `pod_combat_current_hit_chance` either misses (result `2`, logged
   `missed`) or proceeds. No flag = auto-hit, fall through.
4. **Guard** — `pod_combat_check_guard_effect`: if `checks_guard` and
   the target is guarding, consume the guard and block (result `1`,
   logged `guard_blocked`). Else result `0` (hit).

Every non-hit branch sets the result axes and `pod_combat_last_damage =
0`. Only result `0` reaches the payload
(`pod_combat_apply_damage_payload_effect`). HP/contact dispatch and
explicit pure-control delivery consume actor stealth after resolution
regardless of outcome (once per HP AoE burst). The separate morale-only
pipeline consumes the target's stealth/dodge but does not break actor
stealth.

For `requires_control_contact`, result `0` admits the move's hostile
non-damage payloads. Any other result sets the host failure latch; the
normal log outcome remains visible, but story-derived tier/strip/AP/
initiative segments are suppressed because those effects did not land.

#### The class dispatch — damage class → PoD death reason

Every HP-damage move carries a **single-axis damage classification** in
`var:damage_type`: `superficial` (stamped as the default by
`POD_combat_move_set_hp_damage`), `aggravated`, or one of ten elements
(fire / cold / lightning / sun / silver / gold / holy / balefire /
poison / cold_iron; overridden by `POD_combat_move_set_damage_class`, which must run
**after** `set_hp_damage` — the default stamp would clobber an earlier
class write). Before the payload runs,
`pod_combat_apply_single_hp_damage_effect` (the "HP DAMAGE DISPATCH"
banner in `pod_combat_move_effects.txt`) switches on the class and maps
it to a PoD `death_reason` suffix (`$REASON$`), which PoD uses for
splat-weakness multipliers and aggravated classification:

| `damage_type` | `death_reason` |
|---|---|
| `superficial` (the default) or unset | `duel` |
| `aggravated` | `duel_aggravated` |
| `fire` | `fire` |
| `cold` | `cold` |
| `lightning` | `lightning` |
| `sun` | `sun` |
| `silver` | `silver` |
| `gold` | `gold` |
| `holy` | `true_faith` |
| `balefire` | `balefire` |
| `poison` | `poison` |
| `cold_iron` | `cold_iron` |

An **element** rides its real `death_reason` into PoD core, which
decides aggravated / superficial / **immune** **per target** from the
splat-vulnerability matrix in the live
`POD_damage_type_is_aggravated` and
`POD_has_immunity_to_damage_type` triggers
(`common/scripted_triggers/POD_health_track_triggers.txt`).
Immunities, downgrades (e.g. sun-as-superficial), and splat multipliers
all apply. **`aggravated`** forces `REASON = duel_aggravated` for every
target. The elemental mapping is identity in nine of ten cases;
**`holy` maps to `true_faith`**, not `holy_damage`, because PoD keys
aggravated promotion against `true_faith`. Two helper triggers partition
the enum (`pod_combat_triggers.txt`):
`pod_combat_move_class_is_elemental_trigger` (the ten elements) and
`pod_combat_class_always_aggravated_trigger` (`aggravated` / `fire` /
`balefire` — an AI/log heuristic for "aggravated against the typical
target"; the authoritative per-victim verdict is the matrix above, not
this trigger).

The same kind table is mirrored by the weapon-retype switch beside it in
`pod_combat_apply_single_hp_damage_effect`,
`pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` (typed counters) and
`pod_combat_deal_dot_tick_from_host_effect` (DoT ticks) — keep the four
switches in step. To add a class, follow **Adding a new damage class**
in §11.


### Weapon artifact overrides

A move flagged `uses_weapon` swings the actor's 
own equipped weapon, so the artifact in their
`primary_armament` slot can shape the blow. A move may also be granted the
swing for one action by the `uses_weapon` conditional bonus
(`cond_mode = bonus`), which is how "this strike counts as a weapon attack
while X is active" is authored; everything below then applies unchanged for
that action. Only two sites read which of the two it is - the §2c seeder and
the §6 bane/soak marker, both via `pod_combat_move_swings_weapon_trigger` -
so the entire path downstream of the six fight-story transients is shared.
What the artifact can do, read off it as variables stamped at creation:

| Artifact variable | Effect on a `uses_weapon` move |
|---|---|
| `artifact_weapon_damage_class` (flag) | Retypes the damage to that element — but only over a `superficial` move, never over an authored element or `aggravated`, and only against a victim who is not IMMUNE to it. That last clause is checked per victim (`pod_combat_weapon_retype_is_wasted_trigger`) and matters: sun is immune for every splat except vampires, abominations and kuei-jin, so a sun-forged bow would otherwise deal literally nothing to a werewolf and leave its bearer worse off than unarmed. When the retype is wasted the blow falls back to the move's own damage. |
| `artifact_weapon_bonus_damage` (int) | Flat HP added on every target the move reaches. |
| `artifact_weapon_undodgeable` | The move steps around the target's Dodge/Stealth without consuming it (the same semantics as an authored move lacking `checks_stance`). |
| `artifact_weapon_heal_on_damage` (int) | Restores N superficial wound boxes to the wielder when the action draws blood. |
| `artifact_weapon_energy_on_damage` (int) | Restores N **splat-agnostic** energy to the wielder when the action draws blood. PoD scales that to the reader's own pool (`splat_energy_multiplier`, i.e. `maximum_splat_energy / 10`) and ceilings it, so N = 2 is 1 Blood for a vampire and 2 Gnosis for a werewolf. **Author even values** - the display multiplies without a ceiling (there is no ceiling data function), so an odd N renders as a fraction on 5-point pools even though the effect rounds it up. This is the same convention PoD's own energy costs follow (the generator restricts Blood/Gnosis move costs to 0/2/4). The tooltip names the reader's actual resource via `PODEnergySplatIcon` / `PODEnergySplatWord`, the shipped helpers the cost line uses. |
| `artifact_weapon_soak_penalty` (int) | The victim's soak chance drops N percentage points against this weapon's blows, floored at 0. |
| `artifact_deals_aggravated`, or membership in `POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger` | Forces the wound aggravated, per victim. |

**The bane list is per-move inside combat.** `POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger`
(`common/scripted_triggers/POD_weapon_artifact_triggers.txt`) holds the aggravation
LOGIC that promotes damage to aggravated - the generic `artifact_deals_aggravated`
opt-in, the composable `artifact_agg_vs_*` tags, and one named arm
(`blade_of_the_kings`, a power-vs-prowess comparison). Current per-option
carrier counts live in `docs/WEAPON_OVERRIDE_OPTIONS_REFERENCE.md`. PoD's classifier calls it
for every wound its bearer deals; inside the minigame the call is gated on the
actor carrying `pod_combat_weapon_attack`, a marker set only around a
`uses_weapon` move's own damage dispatch. So a bane sword aggravates your
strikes and no longer aggravates your spat venom, your mind blasts, your DoT
ticks or your own recoil. Outside the minigame nothing changed.

**The soak penalty is applied to the roll, not to the number on the card.**
`artifact_weapon_soak_penalty` is read into the fight story's weapon
profile as `pod_combat_weapon_soak_penalty`, then copied at the §6
dispatch site onto the ACTOR character as `pod_combat_weapon_soak_pen`.
Those two names sit four characters apart and have opposite owners: the
fight holds the profile, the attacker holds the bridge. The bridge has to
be character-side because the soak roll happens inside PoD's wound call,
which can only reach the attacker. It rides an actor transient stamped in
the same window as `pod_combat_weapon_attack`, and is subtracted from
`scope:soak_pct` at the
snapshot inside `POD_increase_wounds_effect` - guarded on `scope:combat_host`
plus a nested `trigger_if`, and floored at 0 because nothing downstream
re-clamps. It deliberately does NOT live in `POD_soak_chance`: that script
value is evaluated with no attacker in hand by the slot card, the AI soak
hoist, the character sidebar and the maim roll, so a term there would leak a
lowered soak into all of them. Those surfaces keep showing base soak, which is
the honest answer to "how soaky is this character". The combat log needs no new
surface either - damage is measured from the victim's actual box delta, so a
shredded soak simply shows as a bigger number with a smaller "N soaked" rider.
Riding the marker's exact lifetime is load-bearing: a counter's victim is the
actor themselves, so a leaked penalty would shred their own armour.

**Energy wording is splat-aware everywhere.** Every energy surface names the
resource of the character it actually happens to, via PoD's shipped
`PODEnergySplatWord` / `PODEnergySplatIcon`:

| Surface | Whose resource | How the number is right |
|---|---|---|
| Move card, `energy_gain` bonus and the weapon override line | the reader (`GetPlayer`) | multiplied by `splat_energy_multiplier` in loc, like the shipped cost line |
| Combat log, gain row | the entry's **actor** - an AI's gnosis must not read as the player's blood | `pod_combat_scale_energy_to_splat_effect` converts it at the lift, so the row prints real points |
| Combat log, drain row | the entry's **target** - it is their pool that shrinks | same, scaled to the target |
| Move card, `energy_drain` bonus | nobody - stays generic | the card is drawn before a target exists, so any resource word would be a guess |

The script-side helper exists because loc **cannot ceiling** (there is no ceiling
data function in the tree) while `POD_splat_agnostic_energy_gain` does: 2
agnostic is 1 Blood on a 5-point pool, not 0.9 of one. Anywhere the number is
computed in loc instead (the two card previews), author **even** values - the
same reason PoD's generator restricts Blood/Gnosis move costs to 0/2/4.

**Outside the minigame.** Weapon effects that make sense without a combat
round now apply to ordinary PoD wounds too, keyed on
`POD_is_death_by_weapon` (`common/scripted_triggers/POD_deaths_triggers.txt`) -
the set of death reasons that describe a blow actually struck with something:
duels, brawls, battle, tournament mishaps, assassination. What carries over:

| Effect | Outside the minigame |
|---|---|
| `artifact_weapon_damage_class` | The wound classifies as that element **if the victim is receptive to it** - the `POD_weak_to_*` matrix triggers, shared with the damage table, AND not immune by perk or artifact (`POD_has_immunity_to_damage_type`, the same question the minigame's wasted-retype check asks). An immune victim simply takes the ordinary wound - the element never negates the blow, so a magic sword is never worse than a plain one. |
| `artifact_deals_aggravated` (and the named bane list) | Aggravates weapon-shaped wounds only. A bane sword no longer aggravates its bearer's poisonings, whippings or discipline wounds. |
| `artifact_weapon_soak_penalty` | Subtracted from the victim's soak roll for that packet, same site as the in-fight shred. |
| bonus damage / undodgeable / lifesteal / energy | **Minigame only.** There is no hit roll, dodge stance, or action economy outside a fight to attach them to. |

Two deliberate departures from the obvious implementation, both load-bearing:

- The arms live in `POD_damage_type_is_aggravated`, **not** in the
  `POD_is_death_by_*` wrappers. `POD_is_death_by_fire` also feeds
  `POD_has_immunity_to_damage_type` and two damage-MAGNITUDE script values
  (`POD_wound_multiplier_for_vanilla_death_reason`,
  `POD_wound_multiplier_from_splat_weaknesses`), so folding a weapon arm in
  there would let a fire-immune victim negate an entire sword wound and would
  silently reprice ordinary wounds as fire damage.
- Every outside arm is gated `NOT = { exists = scope:combat_host }`. Inside a
  fight the dispatch already retypes the blow, and every in-fight wound -
  claws, counters, DoT ticks, recoil - carries a live `scope:wound_attacker`,
  so an ungated arm would re-colour all of them.

All three outside arms - damage class, forced aggravation, and the soak shred -
also refuse the case where the attacker IS the victim.
`scope:wound_attacker` is a permanent saved scope that nothing clears, so a
non-hostile wound later in the same effect chain can still see a previous
attacker; the self case is the one that produces visibly absurd results (your
own sword colouring a wound someone else dealt you), and the death-reason gate
does not catch it because PoD's own non-weapon effects pass `REASON = fight`.
A stale *third-party* attacker who also happens to wield a classed weapon is
still possible and is accepted - the fuller fix is a per-packet freshness
marker stamped by the hostile wrappers, which is a larger change to PoD core
than this feature justifies.

Note for reference: PoD's own lethal duels deal `duel_aggravated`, which is
unconditionally aggravated already, so a weapon class does not change them. It
is the non-fatal duel branch and the many `REASON = fight` interaction wounds
that this reaches.

The artifact surfaces render the block **above** the description, matching PoD's
own convention of leading with bold mechanics - `pod_artifact_desc_and_weapon_override`
is `[PODWeaponOverrideSelfText][Artifact.GetDesc]`. The "When wielded:" heading and
the blank line that separates the block from the prose are two dispatchers
(`PODWeaponOvrHeader` / `PODWeaponOvrFooter`) sharing one gate,
`POD_weapon_artifact_has_override_trigger`, which is also what the move card's
and the AI's character-scope check delegates to. That single list is the reason a
newly added override cannot render its line with no heading, which is exactly
what happened when the heading kept its own copy of the var list.

**Adding a weapon is one edit.** Stamp the override variable on the artifact
in its creation effect. Nothing else: no arm in the bane list, no entry in a
name table, no GUI branch, no loc. The runtime, the move card and the AI all
read the same four variables, and the artifact tooltip / details window append
an "When wielded:" block describing them to every override-carrying artifact
automatically (see **Override text** below). The only weapons still named in
script are the ones whose aggravation is *conditional* (on the victim, or on
the weapon's power) - that is logic, not a tag, and it lives in
`POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger`.

The generated text links PoD's own concepts rather than restating them in prose:
aggravation renders `[POD_aggravated_damage|E]` and each element its
`$pod_combat_elemmark_*$` icon, so the player gets the same tooltips the rest of
the mod gives them. The artifacts' hand-written "Converts damage to
[POD_aggravated_damage|E]" sentences have been removed from all 26 descriptions
that carry a bane variable - the block says it now, and cannot drift from the
code. Two were deliberately left alone: Magic Spewer describes an *interaction*
effect rather than a weapon override, and Golden Bow of Artemis is entirely
commented out (its description promises something no script implements -
pre-existing, worth a designer's attention). Thunder of Inoue was originally
left alone for the interaction reason too, but that no longer holds: it carries
`artifact_weapon_damage_class = flag:lightning`, so its retype line IS
generated; only its hand-written sentence about the Storm Winds Slash
interaction stays hand-written.

**Conditional banes are tags too.** A weapon that only savages a particular kind
of prey no longer needs an arm in the dataset file - it carries one or more
`artifact_agg_vs_*` presence variables and
`POD_weapon_artifact_bane_applies_trigger` resolves them:

| Tag | Aggravates against |
|---|---|
| `artifact_agg_vs_vampire` | vampires and abominations |
| `artifact_agg_vs_werewolf` | werewolves |
| `artifact_agg_vs_demon` | demons |
| `artifact_agg_vs_fera` | fera |
| `artifact_agg_vs_wyrm_tainted` | servants of the Wyrm (`POD_is_wyrm_servant_trigger`: the `rf_wyrm` religions incl. Black Spiral Dancers, Viatantabus, the wyrm splat traits, and the tainted-faith set) |
| `artifact_agg_except_werewolf` | everything EXCEPT werewolves (the one inverse). **No artifact carries this since the 2026-08 re-scope turned the klaives blanket**; the tag and its consuming code (the bane trigger arm, the `PODWeaponOvrAggExceptWerewolf` loc segment) are kept for future use. Its migration arms are gone with the rest of the retired migration layer. |

Silver deliberately has **no** tag: `artifact_weapon_damage_class = flag:silver`
already means "aggravated against exactly the splats that are weak to silver",
because that is what the matrix arm says. Quiver of Silvered Arrows carries the
class instead, which is also why the class read falls back to carried kit - a
quiver sits in a trinket slot, not the armament one.

They compose - Ivory Bow sets the vampire and werewolf tags and stacks a line
for each - and every tag has its own emit-or-blank loc segment, so the tooltip
generates the "wounds vampires aggravated" sentence that used to be hand-written
in ten descriptions. Those sentences have been removed; the flavour prose stays.

Only **one** named artifact arm survives in
`POD_weapon_artifact_forces_agg_trigger`: `blade_of_the_kings`, whose condition
is `artifact_power >= victim.prowess` - a power comparison, not a tag, with
nowhere else to live.

**Existing saves are NOT migrated (migration retired 2026-08-08).** Artifact
variables are stamped at creation, so an artifact that already exists in a save
carries whatever it was given when it spawned - a pre-feature artifact has no
override vars at all, and one stamped before a re-scope keeps the old set. A
one-shot backfill plus numbered re-swap effects used to heal this; they were
lost from the tree three times and have been formally retired rather than
restored a fourth. Consequence to design around: the same artifact can behave
differently in two saves depending on when it spawned, and a content re-scope
only reaches new games. Recovery source, if the decision is ever revisited, is
the session transcripts.

**Override text.** `POD_weapon_override_custom_loc.txt` turns those variables
into one standardised line per override, so the move card and the artifact's
own surfaces cannot drift apart. Each segment opens with its own `
`, so
several overrides stack one per line - the same demarcation move effects use.
The composed line reads its weapon from a scope named **`pod_weapon`**; both
callers bind it, the `PODWeaponOverrideSelfText` macro on the artifact surfaces
and a `save_scope_as = pod_weapon` before the tooltip on the move card. On the artifact itself the block rides at the WIDGET level: both
`[Artifact.GetDesc]` surfaces (the artifact tooltip in `gui/shared/cooltip.gui`
and `gui/window_artifact_details.gui`) render
`pod_artifact_desc_and_weapon_override`, which renders the emit-or-blank block
- "When wielded:" header (`PODWeaponOvrHeader`), one line per override, then the
trailing break - ABOVE the description. It CANNOT live
inside a description loc key: `GetDesc` localizes the key in a fresh
environment with no `Artifact` data context (its ROOT is a character - vanilla
descriptions only ever reference `ROOT.Char` or globals), so a
description-embedded `[PODWeaponOverrideSelfText]` errors at runtime and
renders blank. Artifact designers write no mechanical prose and no loc at all;
stamping the variable is the whole job.

**What is deliberately static.** The move card, the sheet `Typ` column, the
compact line's damage marks, the sheet class filters and the AI's class
scoring all read the move's AUTHORED class, because they render off the
shared catalogue story with no reader attached. A retyped move therefore
still reads "superficial" on the card while the combat log's result stamp -
which reports the classifier's real per-victim verdict - shows the truth.
The stat card carries a viewer-relative line instead, in three states: an
unarmed reader gets the generic "Uses Your Weapon" tag; an armed one gets the
weapon named ("Uses Your Weapon - Ivory Bow"); and a weapon carrying overrides
replaces both with a named block ("Uses Ivory Bow:") listing one line per
override ("strikes deal Lightning damage", "+2 damage", "cannot be dodged",
"wounds are aggravated"), built by the same dispatchers the artifact tooltip
uses.

The **`change_type` bonus** (fight `pod_combat_bonus_aggravated`, staged
at §2b) upgrades the blow to aggravated. On a non-elemental move it
simply swaps the `REASON` to `duel_aggravated`. On an elemental move the
element must survive (immunity checks key off it), so the dispatch
instead sets a transient `pod_combat_force_agg` marker on the **actor
character** around the wound call; the devpod delta in PoD's
`POD_damage_type_is_aggravated` reads it through
`POD_wound_attacker_forces_agg_trigger` (a `trigger_if` on
`scope:wound_attacker` with `trigger_else = { always = no }`, so an
attacker-less caller answers no) and force-classifies the elemental wound
as aggravated. The marker drops
immediately after the dispatch (classification is synchronous inside the
wound call), with a belt-and-braces removal in §17.

#### Soak and the wound write

The connecting blow goes through `pod_combat_route_to_pod_damage_effect`, which
shims into PoD's wound pipeline (`POD_increase_wounds_hostile_effect`).
**Soak lives entirely in PoD** — combat passes raw `COUNT`; PoD applies
the soak rolls, the splat-weakness multiplier, the damage cap, immunity,
and the inline death check.

PoD rolls soak **per point**, not once for the whole packet (the soak
revamp; `POD_increase_wounds_effect`). A 4-damage hit against 50% soak
rolls four times, so packets land partially and the common case is chip
reduction rather than a whole-hit coin flip. Expected damage is
unchanged; variance is much lower. Two consequences for combat: a full
soak (every point absorbed) is now rare, and a partial soak looks like a
smaller-than-advertised number unless the log says otherwise.

The route helper returns scope values only (no stored-var side effects):
`scope:damage_dealt` (the actual wound-box/severity delta; 0 whenever no
boxes land, with a fatal path capped to the victim's pre-hit empty
boxes), `scope:attack_result_flag` (`flag:hp_damage` / `soaked` /
`immune`), `scope:result_agg` (PoD's aggravated classification for this
victim + REASON, captured **before** the wound call because a kill wipes
the victim state the classifier reads), and `scope:soaked_count`
(points absorbed, captured before the boxes are written). For a
surviving victim it snapshots superficial and aggravated boxes
separately and takes `max(0, superficial_delta) + max(0,
aggravated_delta)`, so aggravated push-out is not mistaken for a soak.
Morale spillover scales off the actual post-soak damage
(`pod_combat_morale_from_hp_ratio`).

`pod_combat_apply_damage_payload_effect` lifts `scope:soaked_count` into
`pod_combat_last_soaked_count`, which `push_log` stamps onto the entry as
`soaked_count`. `PODCombatLogResultText` then renders a partial-soak
variant of the damage line (the usual `-N` + wound-box icon, followed by
a weak-italic "M soaked" note) so a reduced number reads as soak rather
than a bug. Full soaks still render the bold `Soaked`
outcome, and the two sets are disjoint (the partial branches require
`attack_result = hp_damage` and `damage > 0`).

#### The spirit gate

The spirit gate is the one combat-only protection in the connect step
(PoD has no concept of it). A move carrying `checks_spirit_gate`
against an incorporeal spirit target lands only if the actor carries
the spirit-damage capability; otherwise it no-ops and the row logs as a
miss. Retaliation skips the resolver, so
`pod_combat_apply_retaliation_effect` hand-rolls a gate before its kind
dispatch. In the current implementation **every** counter kind
(morale, aggravated, elemental, or plain) no-ops when the original
attacker is incorporeal and the retaliator lacks spirit damage.

Auto-resolve does not run attacks, so it enforces the gate as a verdict
instead: `pod_combat_player_side_cannot_win_trigger` and its enemy twin
ask whether a side facing a living incorporeal spirit holds any damage
answer at all (`pod_combat_slot_has_spirit_answer_trigger`), and a side
with none cannot win the roll. See *Auto-resolve*. Note the deliberate
asymmetry: morale offense is never gated, and a morale-broken slot does
count as KO'd, so Intimidate really can win a manual fight against a
spirit - the verdict still refuses to count it.

#### The actual result — `scope:result_agg`

What the move *sends* (the class) and what the victim *takes* (PoD's
per-victim classification) can differ — a holy hit is aggravated on a
vampire, superficial on a changeling, and immune on a werewolf or
mundane target. The route helper's
`scope:result_agg` export captures what PoD actually decided; the
payload lift stores it as fight `pod_combat_last_result_agg`, `push_log`
stamps it onto the row as `entry.var:result_agg`, and
`PODCombatLogResultText` picks the aggravated wound-box icon off it, so
the log always matches the health track. The same stamp drives the retal
rider's `retal_agg`. The log shows only the *resulting* damage — element
display lives on the move cards, the sheet, and the tooltips.

