## §3 Slot lifecycle

A slot is one combatant's per-fight state, stored as a `POD_combat_slot`
story_cycle on Charon (`character:POD_wraith_01`). Its identity is the
pair `(combat_team, team_position)` — `combat_team` is `flag:player` or
`flag:enemy`, `team_position` is an int with `0` = leader. There is no
flat slot index. Every slot is reached by iterating the single fight list
`pod_combat_active_slots` and matching that pair (see **Where state
lives** for the addressing model).

Slots are created lazily. A slot story exists only for a filled
position; only a filled slot is appended to `pod_combat_active_slots`;
and only the two team leaders get a fast-path pointer var
(`pod_combat_pc_slot` = player/0, `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot` =
enemy/0), derived once at finalize for the many direct-leader call
sites.

### Creating a slot

`pod_combat_set_slot_char_effect = { TEAM POSITION CHARACTER }` is the
single creation point. It always creates — the duel builder only ever
appends a fresh `(team, position)` from a runtime team-size counter, so
there is no create-guard and no per-position pointer. The body runs
inside a `hidden_effect_new_object` wrap: CK3's tooltip builder walks
effect chains symbolically and skips `create_story`, which would leave a
later `scope:new_slot` read erroring "Undefined event target" during a
tooltip preview. The wrap lets real execution run the new-object block
while preview skips it.

At creation the slot gets:

| Field | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `combat_team` | `flag:$TEAM$` | identity, source of truth for team |
| `team_position` | `$POSITION$` | identity, 0 = leader |
| `slot_alive` | `1` | |
| `slot_char` | `$CHARACTER$` | the occupant; set before `slot_morale` so the starting-morale hook can read it |
| `slot_morale` | `pod_combat_starting_morale_value` | the cap or debug-tuned starting-morale override, plus `pod_combat_starting_morale_modifier_value`, floored at 1. The hook is 0 by default (§11). A fresh slot must read alive before real init because the init-time KO predicate includes `slot_morale <= 0` |
| `slot_energy_used` | `0` | |
| `slot_has_acted` | `0` | |

Soak and prowess are absent entirely. They are sampled from `slot_char`
when a workspace side loads and never stored on the slot; the PoD wound
call remains authoritative for soak. HP gameplay state likewise stays
on the character, although the slot later receives
`slot_mirror_total` / `_agg` / `_maxhp` for display only.

The pure-value stats are zero-defaulted so a script_value gate needs
only a slot-presence check, not a per-field `has_variable`. Gate-style
fields (`slot_hit_chance`, `slot_initiative`, the
stance flags, the tier vars) are deliberately left absent until written.
That absence is itself the signal: presence means "this
stance/tier/effect is active." Remove a field with
`pod_combat_remove_slot_var_effect`; never write a sentinel value for
"off."

### The duel builder

A fight is built imperatively in the PC's scope, then finalized:

```
<PC scope> = {
    POD_combat_create_duel_effect    = { INITIATOR ATTACKER DEFENDER FATALITY FIXED LOCALE OUTPUT_EVENT }
    POD_combat_duel_add_ally_effect  = { CHARACTER }   # 0+ times
    POD_combat_duel_add_enemy_effect = { CHARACTER }   # 1+ times (first → enemy/0)
    POD_combat_finalize_duel_effect  = yes
}
```

`POD_combat_create_duel_effect` creates the `POD_combat_fight` story -
stamping `ctx_host` and `ctx_layout` and carrying the single-combat
handoff data (see **Combat end** for the post-combat handoff) - points
each participant's `pod_combat_session` at it, and seats the PC at
player/0. It sets
`pod_combat_player_team_size = 1` (the PC already occupies player/0) and
`pod_combat_enemy_team_size = 0`. These two counters are the next free
`team_position` for each side.

`add_ally` / `add_enemy` append the next combatant at
`player/<player_team_size>` or `enemy/<enemy_team_size>` and bump that
counter. The first `add_enemy` therefore lands at enemy/0, the primary
opponent and enemy leader (the player team's hit-chance baseline and the
PC's default target). Both adds are soft-capped: an add past
`pod_combat_max_team_size` (50) is silently dropped and the counter is
not bumped, so a dropped seat is detectable by the counter failing to
advance.

`add_*` records occupancy only. Per-slot stat and discipline init is
deferred to finalize, because each combatant's discipline combat buff
needs the opposing team's leader as the buff caster, which is unknown
until the full roster is placed.

### Duel rule: summoning

An optional per-fight rule bars every `summons_combatant` move for both
sides. The launching chain opts in **before** the duel starts:

```
POD_combat_ban_duel_summons_effect = yes
configure_start_single_combat_effect = { ... }
```

`create_duel` reads the opt-in and stamps the fight story's `summons`
var - `flag:allowed` by default, `flag:banned` when barred. Three
scope-specific triggers read it back:
`pod_combat_fight_bans_summons_trigger` (fight scope),
`pod_combat_duel_bans_summons_trigger` (participant character, the shape
the move gates use) and `pod_combat_slot_summons_allowed_trigger` (slot
scope, reaching the fight through the slot's own `fight` back-pointer).
All three fail **open**: no rule var, no fight, and a tooltip build all
read as allowed.

Barred, the move is refused in three places. The shared gate
`pod_combat_move_dynamic_gates_trigger` greys the PC's button with a
reason and drops the move from the AI's candidate pool; the usability
tooltip states the rule; and the summon glue repeats the check before it
creates anything. The auto-resolve spirit verdict also stops crediting an
equipped friendly summon as an answer (**Auto-resolve**).

Event-driven arrivals - `pod_combat_add_*_midfight_effect` and the
scheduled reinforcements - are **not** affected. The rule is about what a
combatant may do, not who may enter.

The opt-in is a saved scope value rather than a builder parameter for two
reasons. The first-fight intro defers the launch into a later event, and
only the scope table crosses that boundary - the same constraint that
forces the fatality ladder in `pod_combat_intro_effects.txt`, which a
second rule would have multiplied. And CK3 has no default for an
unsupplied macro argument, so a parameter would have had to be added to
every one of the ~90 `configure_start_single_combat_effect` call sites.
The value rides the shell card's inherited table into the follow-up, so a
rematch launched from there inherits the bar;
`POD_combat_allow_duel_summons_effect` drops it.

### Finalize

`POD_combat_finalize_duel_effect` completes the build in one atomic tick
— the window opens (`pod_combat_active = yes`) only after the whole
effect returns, so the GUI never sees a half-init roster. In order it:

1. Derives the two leader pointers by scanning `pod_combat_active_slots`
   (player/0 → `pod_combat_pc_slot`, enemy/0 →
   `pod_combat_enemy_leader_slot`) and saves the enemy leader's occupant
   as `scope:pod_combat_target`.
2. Sets `pod_combat_active = yes` and seeds `pod_combat_step_mode` from
   the host PC's per-player step-mode preference (see **Turn loop**).
3. Pre-pass: shapeshifts every AI fera combatant to warform, so each
   potential discipline-buff caster carries its crinos modifier before
   init reads it.
4. Runs `pod_combat_init_all_slots_effect` (per-slot stat / discipline
   init for every occupied slot).
5. Computes leader-baseline hit chances for all slots.
6. Seeds the default PC target (enemy/0) and the workspace target
   pointers.
7. Stamps `pod_combat_nonfatal_active` on every combatant
   (`pod_combat_uses_death_gate_trigger`, which now passes for every
   fatality mode). Practice additionally snapshots every participant's
   wound track for end-of-fight restoration (see **Combat end**).
8. Runs an init-time KO correction: init sets `slot_alive = 1`
   unconditionally, but a combatant who walks in already KO'd (filled
   track, torpor, morale-broken, scripted incap) is flipped down here
   with a bare `set_variable` (deliberately not
   `pod_combat_mark_slot_ko_effect` — a pre-fight correction fires no
   ally-down shock and no log row), then a victory check resolves a
   one-sided start immediately.
9. If combat is not already over, seeds AI loadouts, builds the PC's
   per-character `pod_combat_unlocked_moves` membership list (see **Move
   unlocks**), pushes the "combat begins" log row, rolls round-1
   initiative, and starts the turn loop.

### Move unlocks

Which moves a character may equip and use is a **per-character
membership** fact, held in the `variable_list` `pod_combat_unlocked_moves`
(of `POD_combat_move` story refs) on the viewing / fighting PC. It is
built once per fight (finalize step 9) and once per editor-open by
`pod_combat_bake_one_unlock_effect`, which runs the full unlock
dispatcher per move and appends the passing moves; both builders
`clear_variable_list` first. Every read is a cheap membership test
(`is_target_in_variable_list`) off the per-frame path: the live move
button (`POD_can_execute_combat_move_trigger`), the editor pool
(`pod_combat_loadout_option`), and the loadout sheet
(`pod_combat_sheet_row_match_trigger`) all test the same list. The AI
never reads it — it picks from and validates against its own
`pod_combat_loadout`.

The predicate behind that membership is defined once per move as
`pod_combat_move_<skill_key>_unlock_trigger`. The hidden presentation decision
calls it for failed-requirement text, and the runtime dispatcher calls it
directly on the requested actor. The dispatcher is only the dynamic
story-to-rule adapter; it does not repeat the trait/faith/perk body.
`can_execute_decision` is intentionally absent from runtime unlock checks
because its implicit decision-taker requirements reject generic unlanded
combatants.

The list is per-character **by construction**, so in multiplayer one
player opening the editor never rewrites another player's unlock state
(the editor and a live fight are never simultaneous for one character,
so a single list serves both). `POD_reload_combat_moves` clears it
alongside the loadout and sheet scratch lists; both reload call sites
are additionally gated by `pod_combat_any_fight_active_trigger`, so a
version reload can never `end_story` the shared move catalogue under a
live fight. (This replaced an earlier `pc_can_use` flag baked directly
on the globally-shared move stories, which corrupted a co-op partner's
action bar.)

### Show-all splat filter (display only)

The loadout editor's "Show all skills" lists **locked** moves alongside
unlocked ones, so it is bounded by a second, weaker predicate that must
not be mistaken for the unlock gate above. Each move story carries
`move_unlock_group`, a splat family stamped by `POD_init_combat_move`
from the generator's `config_section` classifier
(`section_unlock_group()`; 16 values, `universal` through `truefaith`).
`pod_combat_move_group_matches_actor_trigger = { ACTOR }` switches on it
and tests the viewer with the matching PoD splat trigger, deliberately
using the widest sensible family so browsing never hides a reachable
move: `has_POD_vamp_blood_trigger` for vampire (ghouls, revenants and
dhampirs learn disciplines) and `POD_splat_has_glamour_trigger` for fae
(kinain hold Arts).

Show-all therefore lists own-family moves **plus** everything already in
`pod_combat_unlocked_moves`, which keeps a multi-splat PC's cross-splat
powers visible. The sub-toggle `pod_combat_show_other_splats` drops the
family restriction and restores the whole ~570-row catalogue; the sheet
renders every row it is given, so that view is slow by nature and the
filter exists to keep the default fast.

It fails open in both directions — an unclassified group and a move
story predating the var both match everyone — because hiding a move a
player could take is a bug while showing one nobody can take is only
noise. The generator refuses to emit an unclassified section for the
same reason.

### Per-slot init

`pod_combat_init_slot_from_character_effect = { CHARACTER ENEMY_LEADER
}` runs in the slot-story scope. It applies the ally/enemy discipline
battle buffs (with the opposing leader as caster), resets `slot_morale`
through `pod_combat_starting_morale_value`, seeds the display-only HP
mirror, and captures the
spirit-capability flags (`incorporeal_spirit`, `can_damage_spirit`). HP,
soak and prowess are **not** snapshotted as gameplay state — wound
accessors read the character, while workspace loads sample prowess and
soak for the current pair (see **Damage pipeline** and §7). The
discipline buffs are character modifiers held to duel end, so later
reads still include them. It also clears per-fight AI scratch (loadout,
anti-repetition history, focus-fire mark, personality precompute) on AI
occupants, and stamps the `pod_combat_duel_active` character modifier
(30-day safety expiry) so other systems can defer mid-duel.

The battery runs **once per seat**, so anything it derives from a
combatant's *current form* is a snapshot. The Delirium is the one such
thing, and `pod_combat_delirium_effects.txt` re-derives it: a combatant
who enters an inducing form later (the PC shapeshifting mid-duel, which
nothing prevents) is picked up by `pod_combat_refresh_delirium_effect` at
four turn boundaries - PC commit, AI turn start, PC turn setup, round end -
and immediately by `POD_combat_on_form_change_effect`, the hook at the tail
of PoD's shapeshift interaction. Casters are the two team leaders only,
matching the `ENEMY_LEADER` the battery is given here. Victims already
carrying either grade are skipped, so the pass is idempotent and each
victim posts one `delirium_struck` log row per fight.

### Mid-fight add and summon

The duel builder runs only pre-combat. To seat a combatant into a live
fight, use the mid-fight entry points in
`pod_combat_summon_effects.txt`:

| Effect | Lands on | When |
|---|---|---|
| `pod_combat_add_ally_midfight_effect = { CHARACTER }` | player team | now |
| `pod_combat_add_enemy_midfight_effect = { CHARACTER }` | enemy team | now |
| `pod_combat_schedule_ally_reinforcement_effect = { CHARACTER JOIN_ROUND }` | player team | start of round N |
| `pod_combat_schedule_enemy_reinforcement_effect = { CHARACTER JOIN_ROUND }` | enemy team | start of round N |

Entry points are team-specific (not one `{ TEAM }`) because CK3 cannot
index the per-team counter, leader pointer, or add-wrapper by a
parse-time token.

A mid-fight seat calls `add_ally`/`add_enemy` to create the slot, then
`pod_combat_init_one_midfight_slot_effect` to run finalize's per-slot
work (init, hit-chance calc, AI loadout seed). The seated combatant gets
`slot_has_acted = 1`, so it sits out the current round and joins the
next round's initiative (where `slot_has_acted` resets to 0). Every
successful seat auto-posts a named "joins the fray" log row, so callers
never wire their own arrival message. The hit/discipline baseline uses
the opposing leader unless that leader is dead, in which case the
lowest-position living opposing slot is used. A practice fight snapshots
the arrival's wound track before seating. Unlike finalize's initial
roster pre-pass, this path does not automatically shift a newly arrived
AI Fera into warform.

The whole seat is gated on the team-size counter actually advancing: a
capped add (past 50) leaves the counter put, and that gate skips the
finalize, the log row, and the death-gate stamp, so nothing
phantom-joins. The mid-fight seat stamps `pod_combat_nonfatal_active`
itself — the finalize-time stamp only covered the initial roster.

Scheduled reinforcements register on the fight list
`pod_combat_pending_reinforcements` (with `pod_combat_join_round` /
`pod_combat_join_team` on the character) and are seated by the per-round
sweep `pod_combat_process_pending_reinforcements_effect` at round end.
`JOIN_ROUND <= pod_combat_round` seats immediately. A reinforcement
killed before its round is dropped, not seated. A due reinforcement is
removed from the pending list before its seat attempt, so a team-cap
failure drops it rather than retrying next round.

Move-driven summons (`summons_combatant`) go through
`pod_combat_summon_combatant_glue_effect`. It refuses outright in a duel
that bars summoning (**Duel rule: summoning**); otherwise it resolves the
friendly or `summon_hostile` destination and capacity-checks that side
before the selected `summon_recipe` creates one initialized character.
Live recipes are `wraith`, `bane`, `gaian_spirit`, and `elemental` (the
elemental recipe uses PoD's Wyrm elemental-bane family). The glue stamps every result
`pod_combat_nonfatal_active` and `spirit_pact_block`, so pact interactions
stay blocked and the flag is there from creation rather than from the
seat, then registers it in `pod_combat_summoned_chars` before seating.
Registration is also what keeps a summon out of the end-of-fight death
sweep (`pod_combat_slot_char_is_summon_trigger`): summons are removed with
`death_disappearance`, never killed.
Both cleanup paths remove every still-living registered summon with
`death_disappearance`; no combat summon persists. The whole body runs in a
`hidden_effect` so the confirm-tooltip walk doesn't error on character/story
creation or list mutation.

### Cleanup

There is no separate slot-teardown effect any more. The fight story's
`on_end` cascades every slot story and every log row, so ending the
fight is the whole teardown -- and a hand-written loop alongside it
would end those stories a second time. `pod_combat_turn_order_list` is
deliberately NOT cascaded: it is a reordered view of the same slot
stories.

`pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect` therefore only does the per-character
work the cascade cannot: strip pending reinforcement state, despawn
summons while their slot references still resolve, and clear the
remaining character-side workspace. See **Combat end** for the full
teardown order.

### Turn loop

A round runs every alive combatant once, in initiative order. The PC
always drives the loop: AI turns before the PC resolve when the round is
set up, the PC acts on player input, then AI turns after the PC resolve
when the PC ends its turn. A 1v1 is just a degenerate NvN (player/0 +
enemy/0), so there is one code path for every roster.

> **Current AI selector contract.** The per-turn action ceiling is the
> effective AP cap + 2 headroom, with an unrestricted fallback,
> one alternate-target retry, and `pod_combat_ai_turn_stalled` as the
> no-progress stop. Controlled turns admit only adverse
> `single_enemy` moves. Initiative debuffs stack; AP drains take the
> strongest pending value; retaliation arming is take-strongest. The
> redundancy gate rejects a candidate only when its entire useful
> payload is redundant, including recipient-aware checks for team and
> ally utility, so useful hybrids remain eligible. Per-action summary
> state feeds finishability, pending drain/control/DoT/initiative
> checks, and recipient-aware healing urgency into admission and
> scoring. HP and morale finish thresholds, kill-priority bands, and
> selector tracing are exposed in the debug tuning window.

#### Initiative

`pod_combat_calculate_initiative_effect` rolls `slot_initiative` for
every alive slot:

```
slot_initiative = pod_combat_initiative_base_value   (the combatant's live prowess, floored at 0, by default; a flat, tunable baseline when the decouple-prowess toggle is on)
                + integer_range[1, pod_combat_initiative_roll_max]   (1..30)
                + slot_init_bonus   (carried from last round's buff/debuff moves, then cleared)
                + pod_combat_initiative_modifier_value   (designer hook, 0 by default - see §11)
```

Tunable knobs (debug tuning window): `pod_combat_initiative_roll_max`
(30) sizes the random roll; the **decouple-prowess** toggle plus
`pod_combat_tune_init_base` (20) swap the base term from prowess to a
flat baseline (via `pod_combat_initiative_base_value`); and
`pod_combat_tune_init_celerity_bonus` adds +N initiative per perk owned
in the Celerity (Vampire) *or* Black Wind (Kuei-jin) speed tree, folded
through `pod_combat_initiative_modifier_value`.
`slot_init_bonus` can be negative (stacked initiative debuffs). The
effect also clears `slot_has_acted` and the round-duration once-per list
`slot_moves_used_round` on every alive slot, then stamps each slot's
display rank (`slot_turn_order`, rank 1 = highest) and rebuilds the GUI
initiative track list `pod_combat_turn_order_list`.

When the debug override
`global_var:pod_combat_tune_init_no_reroll` is set, an existing
`slot_initiative` is retained instead of rerolled; a fresh mid-fight
seat still receives its first roll. The `slot_init_bonus` fold and all
round resets still run. Under that mode, a folded initiative bonus or
debuff becomes a rest-of-fight shift rather than a one-round shift.

Ties break by `(is_enemy ascending, team_position ascending)` — player
team before enemy team, lower positions first. This is a direct
two-field compare, not a single ordinal, so it stays correct for
unbounded teams. The same tiebreak is used by both the rank stamp and
`find_next_actor`, so the initiative track's predicted order is
authoritative even after a reinforcement appends a player slot past the
enemy slots.

#### Finding the next actor

`pod_combat_find_next_actor_effect` walks `pod_combat_active_slots` and
picks the alive, not-yet-acted slot with the highest `slot_initiative`,
breaking ties on the same `(is_enemy, team_position)` key. It writes
`pod_combat_current_actor_team` / `pod_combat_current_actor_position`,
or clears both if every alive slot has acted (round over). The
running-best initiative seeds at `-999`, not `-1`, because a heavily
debuffed slot's initiative can go negative and a `-1` floor would steal
its turn.

#### The atomic AI step

`pod_combat_advance_one_ai_turn_effect` is the indivisible unit: it runs
`find_next_actor`, then `pod_combat_execute_ai_turn_effect` for exactly
one AI actor — guarded so it never executes if combat is over or if
`find_next` landed on the PC (player/0). That PC guard matters only in
the pre-PC context, where the unacted PC can legitimately be the
highest-initiative slot; without it a bare execute would run the PC's
turn as AI. An ally AI at player position > 0 is a genuine AI step and
still executes.

Two drains body this step:

- `pod_combat_run_remaining_ai_turns_effect` — post-PC loop. `while`
  over the alive+unacted+not-over set, calling the atomic step.
  Terminates because each step marks one slot acted.
- `pod_combat_run_pre_pc_ai_turns_effect` — combat-start / round-start
  loop. Walks AI actors until the PC's slot comes up, leaving
  `current_actor` on the PC (then runs
  `pod_combat_setup_pc_turn_effect`), or cleared if the round exhausts.
  It primes its own single `find_next` so the `while` limit can read
  `current_actor` on the first pass. A caller's earlier `find_next` is
  a harmless, idempotent re-selection; finalize and round end both use
  that pattern.

Both drains are additionally bounded by
`pod_combat_ai_outer_turn_cap_value` (shipped default 200). Normal
termination comes from marking each slot acted; if the cap is reached
with an alive unacted slot, the loop emits a debug diagnostic instead
of hanging.

That cap has a tooltip consequence: CK3 renders a counted `while`
body once per allowed iteration when it builds a description, so any
duel-starting option or interaction that reached the pre-PC drain
unhidden printed the loop's innards 200 times. The finalize-side call
is therefore wrapped in `hidden_effect`, and the single-combat
launcher (`pod_combat_sc_launch_minigame_effect`) hides its whole
build chain behind one `custom_tooltip = pod_combat_duel_begins_tt`.
Any new caller that runs a counted loop inside a tooltipped context
needs the same treatment.

#### One AI turn

`pod_combat_execute_ai_turn_effect` runs one actor's full turn: detect a
mind-controlled (stolen) turn, run the per-AI personality precompute
once, select a target (controlled-aware), load the workspace, record the
focus-fire mark, set AP, then run the moves via
`pod_combat_ai_take_turn_effect`. Round buffs clear before workspace
load. Each action attempt saves immediately after execution, after an
unrestricted fallback, and after an alternate-target retry, so KO and
victory state is committed before another action can begin. The final
save is followed by round-debuff expiry, leftover-AP storage,
single-turn control consumption, the acted marker, and a victory check.

The target roll (`pod_combat_ai_select_target_core_effect`) weights base
1, +50, a **x3 kill priority**, +40 cross-turn focus-fire and the
temperament preferences. The kill factor is ONE modifier over an OR of an
HP arm and a morale arm, each gated on the actor being able to deal that
kind of damage (`pod_combat_ai_can_dmg_hp` / `_mor`) - never two stacking
factors, which would reward a state rather than an opportunity. Its
admission set lives in `pod_combat_ai_target_candidate_trigger`, shared by
the roll and by the trace's candidate count so the two cannot drift.
Budget: worst non-kill 116 against 153 for a kill, a 37-point margin. After the moves it saves the
workspace, marks the slot acted (consuming any one-shot control marker),
and runs the victory check.

Each action attempt begins with two target checks, then resolves the move
in three steps - ADMIT, SCORE, ROLL.

0. **Re-target, if needed.** `pod_combat_ai_refresh_target_if_dead_effect`
   replaces a mark that died, then
   `pod_combat_ai_snap_to_finishable_target_effect` moves the mark onto a
   finishable enemy when one exists and the current mark is not one. The
   snap recomputes the finishable summary at its own site (the previous
   action's copy is stale, and on a turn's first attempt belongs to the
   previous ACTOR), restricts the selector's pool to finishable slots for
   exactly one call. `pod_combat_ai_snap_blocked` does not generally
   limit snapping to once per turn; it prevents the pathological
   snap/retry oscillation after an alternate-target retry. Both run
   BEFORE the intent chooser, so the whole move pipeline sees the final
   target.

1. **Intent.** `pod_combat_ai_choose_intent_effect` picks one tactical
   intent (pressure / break / setup / stabilize), `steal` for a
   controlled turn, or `none` for the unrestricted lock/fallback, and
   writes the fight vars `pod_combat_ai_intent` / `_intent_n` /
   `_intent_emg` / `_intent_locked`.
2. **Admit + score.** `pod_combat_ai_score_loadout_effect` walks the
   actor's loadout once. Per candidate it evaluates
   `pod_combat_ai_move_admissible_trigger` (`pod_combat_triggers.txt`) -
   the whole gate set including the intent ladder, and the definition of
   "the pool" - then stamps `pod_combat_ai_admitted` and computes
   `pod_combat_ai_score` on the move story. It folds the fight vars
   `pod_combat_ai_score_max` / `_admitted_n` / `_band_floor`.
3. **Roll.** The `random_in_list` limit is the admitted flag plus the TOP
   BAND (`score >= band_floor`, zero-AP moves exempt); its weight is the
   precomputed score.

Admission and the band both sit at the LIMIT layer, evaluated BEFORE any
weight, so either overrides anything expressed as a score bonus.
`@ai_intent_finish_focus` exists because finish priority was one of those.

The score is the candidate weight formula computed one step earlier -
base, all modifier arms, and its floor - which is what makes a band
possible at all: a weight cannot be compared against the pool's best
while it is still being computed.

`pod_combat_ai_take_turn_effect` is a `while` with
`count = pod_combat_ai_turn_action_cap_value` and a `limit` that stops
early when the pass stalled, combat ended, a controlled turn fizzled, or
the actor itself was downed mid-turn (retaliation / recoil can KO the
attacker between its own actions):

```
while = {
    count = pod_combat_ai_turn_action_cap_value
    limit = {
        NOT = { has_variable = pod_combat_ai_turn_stalled }
        NOT = { pod_combat_is_over_trigger = yes }
        NOT = { var:pod_combat_session ?= { has_variable = pod_combat_turn_control_fizzled } }
        NOT = { pod_combat_current_actor_is_down_trigger = yes }
    }
    pod_combat_ai_try_action_effect = yes
}
```

`count` (the tunable effective AP cap + 2 headroom) is the hard per-turn
action ceiling (hang-safe); the `limit` is the real stop. A `while`
rather than a fixed unroll because net-AP-positive moves can refund AP
mid-turn, so the action count is not fixed. There is deliberately NO
`pod_combat_current_ap_value >= 1` clause: per-move AP affordability is
hoisted to the front of `pod_combat_move_is_usable_now_trigger`, so at 0
AP only the 21 zero-AP moves survive selection, and every one of them is
`once_per`-gated. That gives the AI the same zero-AP behaviour the PC has
always had (C02 parity); an out-of-AP actor with nothing left to cast
picks nothing, which sets `pod_combat_ai_turn_stalled` and ends both this
loop and `try_action`.

#### STEP vs AUTO

Each fight runs in one of two modes, held in the fight var
`pod_combat_step_mode` (present = STEP), seeded at finalize from the host
PC's **per-player** preference `pod_combat_manual_step_pref` (a character
var: present = manual/STEP, absent = auto-advance, the default). The
auto-advance checkbox is per player, not per campaign: its `checked`
binding reads the live per-fight state
(`Not(...pod_combat_step_mode.IsSet)`), and the toggle SGUI flips the
toggling player's own `pod_combat_manual_step_pref` and re-seeds *that*
player's live `pod_combat_step_mode`. So one player toggling never
changes another player's live fight or default. (The former
campaign-global `pod_combat_auto_advance` is retired.)

- **AUTO** drains every pending AI turn in one click, then ends the
  round.
- **STEP** normally resolves one AI turn per click and hands control
  back to the GUI between turns. Two boundary cases are deliberate:
  when round 1 opens with an AI first, finalize resolves none and leaves
  that actor for the first Advance click; at a later round rollover, the
  click that resolves the old round's last AI can also let
  `group_end_round` execute the new round's first pre-PC AI before the
  GUI settles. The settle helpers
  (`pod_combat_after_step_settle_effect` for the post-PC phase,
  `pod_combat_after_pre_pc_step_settle_effect` for the pre-PC phase)
  always re-run `find_next` first so `current_actor` reflects the next
  pending actor before the GUI reads it for the button label, then route
  to PC setup / leave the AI pending / end the round.

#### The two combat-bar buttons

The bottom bar shows one of two buttons, partitioned by whether the PC
has committed its turn:

- **End Turn** (`pod_combat_end_turn` →
  `pod_combat_process_group_turns_effect`) is the PC-commit. It marks
  the PC acted and saves the PC workspace exactly once, then in STEP
  takes the first AI turn and settles, or in AUTO drains the rest and
  ends the round. Disabled mid-AI-turn so a stray click can't compress
  two PC turns into one round.
- **Advance** (`pod_combat_advance_turn`) is the mid-step entry,
  prologue-free by design: it never marks the PC acted or saves the PC
  workspace, so it is safe to click repeatedly while an AI is pending.
  It is mode-aware and routes on whether the PC has acted, so a mid-step
  toggle-to-auto drains on the next click.

Once `pod_combat_is_over` is set, both are replaced by **End Combat**
(see **Combat end**).

#### Round end

`pod_combat_group_end_round_effect` increments `pod_combat_round`,
pushes the round-transition log row, ticks active Damage-over-Time burns
(`pod_combat_tick_dots_effect`, which can itself end combat on a killing
burn — everything after is guarded on `NOT is_over`), seats due
reinforcements, re-derives the Delirium
(`pod_combat_refresh_delirium_effect`, after the seat so a fresh arrival is
not double-counted and before the prowess-derived initiative roll), rolls
next-round initiative, finds the first actor, then sets up the PC
turn or begins stepping/draining the next round's pre-PC AI per mode.

