## §5 Tier system

Buffs and debuffs to a combatant's **soak** and **hit chance** come in
three sizes. Hit chance is rebuilt from a stored pair base plus its
active tiers. Soak has no combat-side base or recalc; PoD's live
`POD_soak_chance` formula folds the character-side tier variables
directly.

### The three tiers

| Tier | Size | Shipped default | Script value |
|------|------|-----------|--------------|
| 1 | small | ±10 | `pod_combat_tier_small_mag` |
| 2 | medium | ±20 | `pod_combat_tier_medium_mag` |
| 3 | large | ±30 | `pod_combat_tier_large_mag` |

The runtime magnitudes are single-sourced as those three script values
in `pod_combat_values.txt`; the debug tuning window can override each
with `global_var:pod_combat_tune_tier_*`. Every runtime consumer reads
the script value by name. Player-facing tutorial text and this reference
still spell out the shipped defaults and must be updated if those
defaults are promoted.

### The eight slots

Each combatant carries up to **eight independent tier values**, one for
every combination of three axes:

- **stat**: `soak` or `hit`
- **direction**: `buff` or `debuff`
- **duration**: `round` (cleared at the holder's own turn boundary, see
  Cleanup) or `combat` (lasts the whole fight)

Each holds `1`, `2`, or `3`, or is absent when that tier is inactive, but
the two stats store them in different places:

- **hit** tiers live on the slot story as
  `slot_hit_<direction>_<duration>_tier`, mirrored into the workspace as
  `pod_combat_<side>_hit_<direction>_<duration>_tier`.
- **soak** tiers live on the COMBATANT as
  `pod_combat_tier_soak_<direction>_<duration>`, with no slot or workspace
  copy. That is the same store `POD_soak_chance` reads, so there is exactly
  one soak tier number per combatant and it is the one the wound roll uses.

### Live hit = base + sum of active tiers (recalc-from-base)

Live hit chance is rebuilt by the recalc effects in
`pod_combat_tier_effects.txt`. They always start from the stored
**base** (`slot_base_hit_chance`, or the workspace
`pod_combat_<side>_base_hit_chance`), then walk the four hit tier
variables and clamp the result. Every tier is a flat percentage point,
**added** for a buff and **subtracted** for a debuff; it clamps to the
live `pod_combat_hit_chance_min` / `_max` values (shipped defaults
`[20, 95]`, debug-tunable).

The output is written back to `slot_hit_chance` (and the workspace
`pod_combat_<side>_hit_chance`). Stealth's hit bonus
(`pod_combat_stealth_hit_bonus`, shipped default +30 and debug-tunable)
folds into the same recalc, keyed off the `*_is_stealthed` flag, so it
survives every rebuild.

Recalc-from-base is the load-bearing rule: live hit chance is **never**
adjusted by deltas. Removing a tier means dropping its variable and
recalculating, so clamping losses cannot accumulate across rounds.

**Soak has no recalc.** Its tiers are folded into `POD_soak_chance`
itself (buffs multiplicative, debuffs flat points - see **Soak bridge to
PoD's wound rolls**), so there is nothing to rebuild and nothing that can
drift from the wound roll. The recalc helpers:

| Effect | Scope | Rebuilds |
|--------|-------|----------|
| `pod_combat_recalc_hit_actor_effect` / `_hit_target_effect` | workspace | actor / target hit (actor also syncs `pod_combat_current_hit_chance`) |
| `pod_combat_recalc_slot_hit_via_story_effect` | slot story | hit chance on the current slot |

### Upgrade-only application

Applying a tier is **upgrade-only**: a higher tier overwrites a lower
one, but re-applying the same or a lower tier is blocked. Every write in
the tier-application step of `pod_combat_move_effects.txt` is
OR-guarded:

```
OR = {
    NOT = { has_variable = <tier_var> }                  # first application
    var:<tier_var> < $STORY$.var:<move's tier>           # strictly higher
}
```

So a tier-1 move can never silently downgrade a stacked tier-3 effect.
The shared redundancy gate rejects a move for this reason only when the
move has no independent useful payload and its entire rider set is
redundant; useful hybrids remain selectable and the inferior tier write
simply no-ops. The write guard also covers bypass paths (console, test
actions). Recalc (hit) and the stash refresh (soak) fire whenever the
move *declared* a tier, not whether the write happened, so a blocked
write leaves the higher tier in place.

The selection-time redundancy gates come in matched pairs, one reading
each store: `pod_combat_tier_upgrade_only_trigger` /
`pod_combat_slot_tier_rider_upgrades_trigger` for hit tiers (workspace and
slot vars), and `pod_combat_soak_tier_upgrade_only_trigger` /
`pod_combat_slot_soak_tier_rider_upgrades_trigger` for soak, which compare
against the occupant's `pod_combat_tier_soak_*` bridge var. Each half reads a
different store through a different hop: the hit gates reach the fight through
`pod_combat_session`, the soak gates reach the combatant through
`pod_combat_<side>_char`. Neither reads its ambient scope, which is the host
CHARACTER at both call paths. Point either at a var that scope does not hold and
it fails **open** with no error, because a `trigger_if` whose limit is false is
skipped and a skipped `trigger_if` contributes nothing to the enclosing block -
which in an AND reads as true, so the AI quietly re-casts buffs it already has.
Keep the pairing intact. Note this is an AND-context rule: vanilla puts bare
`trigger_if` arms straight into an `OR` and relies on a skipped arm NOT making
the OR true (`00_vassal_stance_triggers.txt:59-82`,
`00_activity_triggers.txt:329-352`), so do not read "skipped is true" as
universal. Either way the safe shape is explicit - pair the `trigger_if` with a
`trigger_else` that states the answer, as `POD_wound_attacker_forces_agg_trigger`
does.

Duration is not part of the question. `pod_combat_upgrade_tier_var_effect`
refuses any write that is not a strict raise, so an equal-tier re-apply writes
nothing at either duration, and neither clear is a countdown a re-apply could
postpone: round tiers are stripped on a fixed turn boundary, combat tiers only at
combat end. Round versus combat changes only how much the waste costs, which is
why it is expressed in the AI weight arms (`@ai_round_buff_spam` /
`@ai_round_buff_lateap` on round riders, `@ai_combat_buff_reward` on combat ones)
and nowhere else.

By convention buffs target the actor (`pod_combat_actor_*`) and debuffs
target the victim (`pod_combat_target_*`). A `single_ally` buff or stance
instead targets the selected beneficiary (`pod_combat_target_*`); its
soak tier write and hit recalc follow that ally. PC redundancy gates inspect
the selected ally. The AI chooses its ally after move selection, so its
`single_ally` candidates go to `pod_combat_ai_has_ally_benefiting_trigger`,
which runs the same upgrade tests against each candidate slot's own state.

### Soak bridge to PoD's wound rolls

Tier hit modifiers only matter inside the mini-game, but soak tiers must
reach PoD's real wound rolls. When a soak tier is applied, the
actor/target character also gets a
`pod_combat_tier_soak_<direction>_<duration>` variable. PoD's
`POD_soak_chance` (in `POD_health_track_values.txt`) reads those four
variables, so a soak buff genuinely improves the wound roll the moment
damage is dealt — not just the displayed number. See **Damage pipeline**
for how soak factors into the wound roll.

**Buffs and debuffs use different math, deliberately.**

- **Buffs are multiplicative**, like every other soak source. A tier is a
  constant proportional cut to incoming damage (a large buff always
  multiplies damage taken by 0.7) rather than a flat point gain that
  grows in value the soakier the target already is. On a 0%-soak
  character multiplicative and additive agree (both give 30 for a large
  buff); they diverge on stacked-soak targets, where the old additive
  form was worth far more — 64% soak went to 94% additively, and goes to
  74.8% now.
- **Debuffs stay flat percentage points.** A multiplicative debuff could
  never meaningfully strip a stacked tank (90% soak times a 0.3 factor is
  still 87%), so keeping them subtractive is what makes soak-shredding
  moves the answer to a soak build.

**One formula, no mirrors.** Combat never re-implements this math: it
writes the tier level onto the combatant and reads `POD_soak_chance`
live, so the slot card, every AI soak threshold, and the wound roll are
by construction the same number. (This replaced a design where the combat
side kept its own copies of the formula and had to be held in lockstep
with it.)

The buff block still works in percentage space — flip to `100 - soak`,
apply the tier factors, flip back — rather than folding into the product
further up, because the flat Fae terms (`POD_quest_of_deed_modifier`,
`cantrip_deja_vu_modifier`) have already landed by that point, so a
product-phase factor would not give the same answer. The 0-100 clamp
before the bridge is load-bearing for the same reason: the flip assumes a
0-100 input. Hit tiers are unaffected — plain percentage points both
ways, in `pod_combat_recalc_hit_core_effect`.

### Cleanup

Round-duration tiers expire at each combatant's **own turn boundary**, not at
round end: a buff clears at the start of the holder's turn (so a self soak buff
guards the whole enemy phase, then lapses when the holder acts again); a debuff
at the end of the holder's turn (so it afflicts its victim through that turn).
Combat-duration tiers persist to combat-end cleanup.

| Effect | When | Clears |
|--------|------|--------|
| `pod_combat_clear_actor_round_buffs_effect` | acting combatant's **turn start** | that slot's `hit_buff_round_tier` + the occupant's `pod_combat_tier_soak_buff_round`, then recalcs the slot's hit |
| `pod_combat_clear_actor_round_debuffs_effect` | acting combatant's **turn end** | that slot's `hit_debuff_round_tier` + the occupant's `pod_combat_tier_soak_debuff_round`, then recalcs the slot's hit |
| `pod_combat_clear_combat_tiers_effect` | combat end | all four slot hit tier vars + all four soak tier vars on each occupant |
| `pod_combat_init_slot_from_character_effect` | slot init | the incoming combatant's four soak tier vars (see below) |

The two round clears find the acting slot by the `current_actor` team/position
match (called from the turn-setup / turn-end sites in
`pod_combat_group_effects.txt`); the combat-end clear sweeps every filled slot.
All run in slot-story scope, with `var:slot_char` switching into the participant
character for the soak tiers.

**Every soak-tier clear is `is_alive`-gated**, because var mutation on a dead
character errors. A combatant killed mid-duel therefore keeps its soak tier vars,
and two things cover that: `POD_soak_chance` gates all twelve bridge branches on
`is_alive`, so a corpse's leftovers never affect anything read off it; and slot
init clears the four vars on the way *in*, so a character who is later brought
back cannot carry a stale tier into a new fight. Do **not** try to strip them in
`POD_on_death` — root is already dead there, so the removes error and do nothing.

**Known gap, accepted:** a KO'd combatant never becomes current actor, so its
round-duration tiers are not cleared at a turn boundary and lapse only at combat
end. Nothing observable depends on it (KO'd slots are untargetable and take no
wound rolls).

### `team_all`: the both-teams sweep

`targeting = team_all` hits **every living combatant, the caster included**,
on both sides. It reuses the `team_enemy` machinery wholesale — per-victim
hit rolls, one burst-level stealth consume, one log row per victim.

**The caster is not swept; it is hit actor-side afterwards.** The loop's
filter deliberately skips it:

```
pod_combat_aoe_team_all_sweep_filter_trigger = {
	NOT = { pod_combat_slot_is_current_actor_trigger = yes }
}
```

This is a correctness requirement, not a style choice. Sweeping the caster
would load the same combatant into the **target** workspace while it is also
the **actor**, and `pod_combat_save_workspace_effect` flushes the actor
workspace back over every mirrored slot field at end of move — so the loop's
writes for the caster would be silently reverted. `slot_morale` is the clearest
casualty; HP would appear to work only because it lives on PoD's wound track
rather than the slot. (The same hazard is latent in `team_ally`, whose sweep
*does* include the actor: a `team_ally` morale heal on the caster is lost the
same way.) Because the matcher derefs `scope:host.var:pod_combat_session`,
every caller must save the host first, and the matcher itself is
`trigger_if`-guarded on the pointer - in a trigger `?=` does not skip an
unset link (see **`?=` behaves differently in triggers than in effects**).

So each applier sweeps the others, then applies the caster's share to the actor
side: typed HP damage via `pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` (bypass
guard, no hit roll — you neither block nor miss yourself — but PoD still soaks
and classifies it, so a caster immune to its own element shrugs it off),
morale damage via `SIDE = actor`, and debuff **tiers** via
`pod_combat_apply_team_all_self_debuffs_effect`. The caster's HP share is lifted
into `pod_combat_last_self_damage`, so the existing recoil rider on the §16
wrap-up row reports it; the lift is additive, since the move may also carry a
`self_damage` rider.

`bypass_dodge` / `bypass_stealth` are **not** self-applied: they describe how
the attack meets each victim's defenses rather than a debuff the blast inflicts,
and breaking the caster's own stealth in the pre-damage phase would rob the
burst of the stealth accuracy it is entitled to.

Three further consequences worth stating:

- **Damage and debuffs broadcast; support does not.** §5b broadcasts tier
  debuffs and stance-strips across the sweep, but §5c/§9/§10/§12/§12b route
  buffs, heals and beneficiary stances to the **actor** for every mode
  except `single_ally` / `single_pc`. A `team_all` heal would therefore mend
  only its caster, so the generator's `team_all_support` lint rejects the
  combination rather than shipping the surprise.
- **No reactions.** §15 skips `team_all` for the same reason it skips
  `team_enemy`: a counter can only fire from one workspace target, so firing
  would arbitrarily single out the pre-AoE one.
- **Victory is scored after the move, not mid-sweep.** A burst that KOs the
  last enemy keeps sweeping the remaining allies. The ally-down morale shock
  fires per KO inside the loop but is floored at 1 morale, so it can never
  cascade a second KO.

For the AI this is a last-one-standing button: `@ai_aoe_multi_enemy` still
rewards a crowded enemy side, but three stacking friendly-fire penalties bury
it otherwise — `@ai_aoe_ff_self` always applies (the caster is always in the
blast), and `@ai_aoe_ff_one_ally` / `@ai_aoe_ff_two_ally` add on top while the
actor still has living teammates.

