## §6 Reaction system

A **reaction** is a counter-strike a combatant arms on its own turn and
that fires automatically on the next matching eligible incoming
single-target HP-damage attack. Pure morale/control/utility moves and
`team_enemy` / `team_all` attacks do not check reactions. Three flavours
exist:

- **Thorns** — fires when an attack makes **contact** (lands, is soaked,
  or is guard-blocked).
- **Riposte** — fires when an attack **misses** (whiffs, is dodged, or
  hits stealth).
- **Always** — fires on **any** resolved attack, hit or miss.

The Always charge gates on `outcome = flag:attack` alone — any
`attack_result`, `immune` included; it is not a member of the
contact/evasion partition but the union of the whole axis, so any
resolved swing (landed, blocked, soaked, evaded, or shrugged off by
immunity) consumes it.

If a specific charge (hit or miss) and the Always charge are both armed
when an attack resolves, the specific charge answers it and the Always
charge is held for a later attack — at most one counter fires per
incoming attack.

Take-strongest replacement compares candidates within one charge family
only: a stronger Always candidate replaces only the armed Always charge
and never displaces — nor is displaced by — a hit- or miss-charge.

The lifecycle has two halves: arm now, fire on the next matching
eligible attack.

### Arming (during the arming combatant's own move)

In the move-execution step that handles retaliation stances
(`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`), a move with `retaliate_if_hit`,
`retaliate_if_missed`, or `retaliate_if_attacked` writes the armed
reaction onto the actor's workspace, which is mirrored onto its slot
story:

| Move var | Workspace var armed | Fires on |
|----------|--------------------|----------|
| `retaliate_if_hit` | `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_hit` | contact |
| `retaliate_if_missed` | `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_miss` | evasion |
| `retaliate_if_attacked` | `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_any` | any resolved attack |

The candidate counter damage is chosen as `retaliate_damage` →
`hp_damage` → `1`; an arming move therefore always supplies an amount.
Arming is take-strongest: the candidate replaces an existing reaction
only when its amount is strictly larger. An equal or smaller candidate
leaves both the amount and its current kind unchanged. Three companion
vars describe the counter, each with a `_hit` / `_miss` form:

| Companion var | Meaning |
|---------------|---------|
| `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_<hit\|miss\|any>_kind` | counter's damage kind: `morale`, `aggravated`, or an elemental class flag (fire/cold/…); unset = plain |
| `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_<hit\|miss\|any>_kind_from_weapon` | presence companion marking the kind as WEAPON-derived. Set only by the weapon arm; read by the fire-time waste guard. Mirrors to the slot with the other two - a six-site lockstep field |
| `pod_combat_actor_retaliate_<hit\|miss\|any>_morale` | presence companion to a `morale` kind (drives the slot tooltip) |

Kind precedence is **morale > `retaliate_agg` > the move's elemental
class > the equipped weapon's retype > unset**: a `retaliate_morale` move
sets the `_kind` to `morale`; a `retaliate_agg` move sets it to
`aggravated`; otherwise an elemental move's damage class seeds it
(`pod_combat_seed_kind_from_class_effect`, literal flags only); failing
that, a live weapon retype seeds it and stamps `_kind_from_weapon`; a
plain move with no weapon leaves it unset.

**Weapon-derived kinds stand down at fire time.** The kind is stamped when
the counter is ARMED, but it fires during a later attacker's dispatch - by
then the live weapon profile belongs to the attacker, so the direct-hit
guard cannot be reused. `pod_combat_stamped_type_is_wasted_trigger` reads
the STAMPED kind instead, and if the counter's victim is immune to it the
kind and its provenance marker are stripped so the plain-duel branch runs.
Only weapon-derived kinds stand down: an AUTHORED elemental counter is the
move's identity and may legitimately read immune. Consequence to know: an
armed-stance tooltip can advertise an element the counter will not use,
because the victim is unknowable until it fires.

The slot story carries the same fields as `slot_retaliate_hit` /
`slot_retaliate_miss` / `slot_retaliate_any` (+ the `_kind` / `_morale`
companions), which drive the per-slot retaliation status icon
(`pod_combat_slot_has_retaliation_value`) and its tooltip
(`PODCombatStatusRetaliationText`).

### Firing (during the next attacker's move)

For a non-`team_enemy`, non-`team_all` move carrying `hp_damage`, the
reaction phase calls `pod_combat_check_retaliation_effect` with
`TARGET_SIDE` = the side holding the stance (usually `target`) and
`ACTOR_SIDE` = the attacker who will take the counter. It checks the
three armed slots **in this order**:

| Armed var | Gated by | Fires on |
|-----------|----------|----------|
| `pod_combat_<side>_retaliate_hit` | `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_contact_trigger` | `hp_damage` / `soaked` / `guard_blocked` |
| `pod_combat_<side>_retaliate_miss` | `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_miss_trigger` | `missed` / `dodged` / `stealthed` |
| `pod_combat_<side>_retaliate_any` | `pod_combat_reaction_any_condition_trigger` | any resolved attack the two above did not answer |

The first two **result-code partition triggers** read the just-resolved
`pod_combat_last_outcome` / `pod_combat_last_attack_result` (see
**Result model**) and decide which reaction is eligible. The third gate
wraps `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_attack_trigger` (the union of
the whole attack axis) with `NOT = { has_variable =
pod_combat_last_retal_fired }`, which is what implements the hold rule —
so the call order above is semantics, not style. The shared helper
`pod_combat_apply_reaction_if_triggered_effect` runs the
limit-then-fire-then-clear pattern once per slot, then removes the armed
var and both companions in lockstep (so a stale companion can't
mislabel a later counter). A matching attack consumes the reaction even
if the counter's later spirit gate makes its payload no-op.

### How the counter resolves

`pod_combat_apply_retaliation_effect` deals the counter. A retaliation
is a **reflex**, not a deliberate attack, so it skips the contact
resolver and the hit roll entirely, and uses the bypass-guard damage
path (guard protects against incoming attacks, not counters). It only
hand-rolls the **spirit gate** before kind dispatch: every counter kind
against an incorporeal spirit no-ops unless the retaliator can damage
spirits, which preserves the main attack's log line.

It dispatches on the counter kind in priority order **morale > typed >
plain**:

| Branch | Effect | Damage routing |
|--------|--------|----------------|
| morale (`_kind` = `morale`) | erodes `pod_combat_<actor>_morale`, capped at current morale; does **not** consume the victim's stance | logs the actual morale lost; sets `pod_combat_last_retal_morale` |
| typed (`_kind` = `aggravated` or an elemental class) | `pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` maps the class to a PoD `death_reason` (`aggravated` → `duel_aggravated`, `holy` → `true_faith`; the same kind table as the direct-hit and DoT switches) and deals it bypass-guard | sets `pod_combat_last_retal_agg` from the route helper's actual `scope:result_agg` (PoD's per-victim classification), not the armed class |
| plain (`_kind` unset) | bypass-guard HP via `REASON = duel` | — |

Every branch records the counter's damage in
`pod_combat_last_retal_damage` (HP branches store the post-soak amount;
morale stores the capped loss) and finally calls
`pod_combat_set_retal_fired_effect`, which sets
`pod_combat_last_retal_fired`. That overlay flag is independent of the
main outcome, so a missed main attack with a landed counter shows both
`attack_result = missed` **and** the retal rider. See **Combat log** for
how the counter renders as a rider on the row.

