## §7 Engine constraints

These are the CK3-engine rules the combat code is shaped around. Each is
a standing constraint, not a suggestion: violating one produces silent
wrong behavior, not a compile error. Read this section before
"improving" anything that touches the slot loops, the workspace, or a
customizable_localization renderer.

### Var mutation on dead characters errors

`remove_variable` / `clear_variable_list` on a **dead or removed**
character reference errors ("scope does not have variables"); the same
op on a *living* character with no variable list is a tolerated no-op.
Read-only triggers (`is_alive`, `is_ai`) still resolve on a
dead-but-present scope, so any cleanup loop that mutates combatant
characters guards with `is_alive = yes` first (tier cleanup, the AI
scratch clear, the pending-reinforcement strips, the host-death
release). KO'd is not dead: a KO'd-but-living combatant still gets its
per-fight vars cleared.

### The action workspace is a persistent current-pair cache

The workspace alias pair (`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*`
on the fight story) is an execution adapter, not the durable home for
every combatant. It holds the currently loaded actor and target. A full
load establishes the pair at turn setup; target-only loads repoint it
for swaps and AoE iterations. A typical turn looks like:

```
pod_combat_load_workspace_effect       ← establish actor + target
    (one or more actions; target may be reloaded between them)
pod_combat_save_workspace_effect       ← write mirrors back
    (workspace remains loaded and may serve the next action)
```

`pod_combat_save_workspace_effect` does **not** clear or invalidate the
workspace. PC move buttons can execute and save repeatedly without a
new load, and AI target changes reload the pair only when needed. The
cache becomes stale only when code changes its underlying slot directly,
changes the selected pair without loading it, or moves to a new turn.
To answer "what is some non-loaded slot's current morale / stance /
tier", read that slot story directly. The load/save effects are the
authorized translators between slot state and its current workspace
mirror. See **Where state lives** for the full owner model.

**A direct slot-field write that reaches a live actor/target must mirror
back.** Writing a `slot_*` field straight on a slot story (e.g. the
ally-down morale shock's `slot_morale` write) does not touch the
workspace. If that slot happens to be the currently-loaded actor or
target, the next `pod_combat_save_workspace_effect` flushes the stale
pre-write workspace value back over the slot, silently erasing the direct
write (a dominated actor KO-ing a teammate on its final AP would
otherwise lose its own shock). So such a write mirrors the new value into
`pod_combat_actor_*` / `pod_combat_target_*` on the fight story when the
slot is the current actor / selected target —
`pod_combat_apply_ally_down_shock_to_slot_effect` does this, gated by
`pod_combat_slot_is_current_actor_trigger` /
`pod_combat_slot_is_selected_target_trigger`.

**AoE dispatchers reload only the target, not the whole workspace.** The
AoE tails call `pod_combat_reload_selected_target_effect` (a
target-only reload) rather than the full
`pod_combat_load_workspace_effect`, so the live actor workspace (spent
energy, consumed stealth, applied tiers) survives the sweep; only the
target half is re-pointed, and `load_target → recalc_pair_hit_chance`
repairs the actor hit chance the per-target loop transiently corrupted —
without an actor reload. The roll-based enemy-HP AoE additionally defers
its actor stealth-consume to one post-loop consume via the fight tripwire
`pod_combat_aoe_suppress_stealth_consume`, so every swept target keeps
the +30 stealth accuracy before stealth breaks once.

**Enforcement is convention-only.** There is no live checker in the
active tree. A debug assert that validated workspace/slot sync exists
only in `devpod-old/` as a porting reference; the
`pod_combat_debug_active` / `pod_combat_debug_workspace_desync` flags
stay validator-suppressed for that purpose. Adding a mirrored field to
one of the load/save helpers without adding it to the other is the most
likely source of silent workspace-slot drift (see **Extending the
system** → adding a workspace mirror field).

### Scope traps inside slot loops

> **Log pushes belong to the host, not the slot.** Both
> `pod_combat_push_system_log_effect` and `_named_effect` do
> `save_scope_as = combat_host` on **their caller**. Calling one from inside a
> loop body that has switched into `var:slot_char` therefore re-points
> `scope:combat_host` at that combatant, and every later matcher in the running
> AI turn reads the wrong character. Hop back explicitly - save the host under a
> name of your own before the walk and push through it, as
> `pod_combat_apply_delirium_to_victim_effect` does.


Three engine behaviors govern every body that runs inside `every_in_list
= { variable = pod_combat_active_slots ... }` (and the DoT tick loop).
They are load-bearing for any new code in those loops.

**An inline scope switch corrupts the iteration scope for later inline
reads.** After an inline `scope:X = { }` or `var:slot_char = { }` switch
in a loop body, subsequent **inline** statements read the iteration
element's own vars as unset. Scripted-effect **calls** stay reliable —
their bodies read `this` correctly. So every AoE and DoT loop body is
shaped the same way: **do all slot-local reads/writes first, then do
contained calls and scope switches last.** The char-bridge soak writes
in the AoE buff/debuff appliers (`var:slot_char = { ... }`) are
deliberately the final statement in the loop for exactly this reason.

Concretely, never deal wounds from slot scope. HP damage routes through
the workspace round-trip (load target from story → call the damage
helper inside `scope:host = { }` → save back → KO-check). See **Damage
pipeline**.

**Flag-valued vars degrade to plain values when lifted into a scope
value.** `save_scope_value_as` reading a flag-holding variable yields a
`value`-typed result. Every later `= flag:X` comparison on that result
is then a **type error, not a `false`**, so `if`, `else`, and `NOT` all
silently skip; a trigger and its negation can both fail, and the error
usually dedupes to invisibility after the first occurrence.

**Variable-to-variable copies keep the flag type.** Both
`set_variable = { name = A value = var:B }` and the object-deref form
`value = scope:slot.var:combat_team` preserve it. The turn engine rests
on this: `pod_combat_find_next_actor_effect` copies `combat_team` off
the winning slot into `pod_combat_best_team` and then into
`pod_combat_current_actor_team`, which `pod_combat_is_player_turn_trigger`
compares against `flag:player` on every turn of every fight. Saving a
flag **literal** as a scope value is likewise fine — that is how the duel
rule flags ride the shell card (§10). The degrading path is specifically
variable → scope value. The rule:

- Assign flags as **literals** wherever a literal will do, and never lift
  one into a scope value. The seeding pattern is a default
  `set_variable = { value = flag:duel }` followed by independent `if`
  branches that compare the **source** var and overwrite with a
  literal. See `pod_combat_seed_kind_from_class_effect`.
- To move a flag across scopes, read it through a saved **object**
  (`scope:obj.var:flag_var = flag:X`), never a scope value.
- Numbers are immune — only flags degrade. Lifting `dot_amount` /
  `dot_rounds` into scope values is fine.

Read-side dispatch is safe even on flags, because a `switch` (or `if`)
**compares** the source var rather than copying it. The DoT host
dispatch, DoT tick-message dispatch, typed-retal dispatch, and the
move-unlock dispatcher use `switch` for this reason.

**A `var:X` read errors when `X` is unset.** Reading
`var:slot_dot_rounds` (etc.) on a slot that has no such variable raises
an error, not a default. Guard every potentially-absent read with
`has_variable` (or filter the iteration `limit` on it). Many slot fields
are deliberately absence-as-signal (`slot_init_bonus`, `slot_ap_drain`,
`slot_dot_*`, the tier vars, stance flags) — see **Slot lifecycle** for
the full list.

### Character stats stay authoritative; current-pair reads may be hoisted

Current HP, max HP, and aggravated/total wound counts are read live from
each slot character's PoD wound vars (`POD_empty_damage_boxes` /
`POD_total_damage_boxes` / `POD_health_aggravated_damage_num`) through
the slot HP script values. The PoD wound pipeline is authoritative; the
slot story holds only the `slot_char` pointer, never a cached HP number.
A cache on the slot would desync against PoD's wound writes between
turns, so HP stays derived on every read. See **Variable reference** for
the slot HP SVs and **Damage pipeline** for the single write path
(`POD_increase_wounds_*`).

**Soak and prowess remain character-authoritative but are hoisted for
the loaded pair.** `pod_combat_stash_side_live_stats_effect` samples
`POD_soak_chance` and prowess when a side loads, then AI scoring and
workspace consumers reuse those values until the next refresh. Soak is
also refreshed after a move writes a soak tier, and the actual PoD wound
call evaluates its own authoritative soak path. This keeps wound-track
prowess penalties and live soak changes feeding the fight without
re-walking expensive script values for every candidate move.

**The combat window pins the game clock**, so no daily skill recalculation
ever lands during a fight. Any code that adds or removes a character
modifier mid-fight must therefore call `force_character_skill_recalculation`
on that character itself, or no `prowess` read - the stash, the hit
formula, initiative, auto-resolve, the slot card - will ever see it.

The one sanctioned exception is the **wound-bar display mirror**
(`slot_mirror_total` / `slot_mirror_agg` / `slot_mirror_maxhp`): the
combat window's wound pips read these plain slot vars for perf instead
of re-deriving the SV chain ~80x per slot card per frame. They are
display-only and rewritten at every HP-change chokepoint (the
`pod_combat_save_workspace_effect` and `pod_combat_tick_dots_effect`
tails, plus per-slot seeding at init) before the GUI next renders, so
they cannot go stale. Game logic and the AI never read the mirror. Do
not point the GUI back at the live SVs — that reintroduces the per-frame
fanout this mirror exists to remove.

### DoT ticks at round end and must respect the slot-loop rules

A burn (`slot_dot_amount` / `slot_dot_rounds` / `slot_dot_kind` /
`slot_dot_source`) ticks once per round in
`pod_combat_tick_dots_effect`, called from
`pod_combat_group_end_round_effect` after the round increment and before
reinforcement seating / initiative recalc. The tick loop body is ordered
into phases precisely to obey the scope-trap rules above:

1. **Slot-local first:** lift the tick amount into scope values, save the
   victim scope for the log push, resolve the burn's attacker into
   `scope:dot_attacker` — the caster (`slot_dot_source`) if it is still
   alive, else the victim itself (an always-valid scope that never
   mis-credits a live third party) — then decrement `slot_dot_rounds`.
   All before any inline scope switch.
2. **The damage, next:** a morale burn erodes `slot_morale` slot-locally;
   an HP burn runs the same workspace round-trip as damage AoEs (load
   target → `scope:host = { pod_combat_deal_dot_tick_from_host_effect }` →
   save back → KO-check). The host dispatch maps kind → REASON and passes
   `ATTACKER = scope:dot_attacker`. Both paths export the **actual**
   applied amount as `scope:dot_boxes_applied` (0 on a fully-immune /
   clay-man HP tick; the capped loss on a morale tick). HP ticks also
   export `scope:dot_result_agg` from PoD's victim-specific classifier
   and `scope:dot_result_immune` from its hard-immunity branch.
3. **Resolve-then-log:** `pod_combat_push_dot_tick_resolved_log_effect`
   pushes the tick row **after** the wound resolves, so it reports
   `dot_boxes_applied` — the true landed amount, not the raw seeded number
   — and uses `dot_result_agg` for the wound-box icon. Hard immunity gets
   its own immune row; another zero-damage result, such as clay-man
   protection, gets a neutral blocked row and is never called Soak. It is
   a scripted-effect call reading only saved scopes, so it stays reliable
   after phase 2's inline switches.
4. **Expiry, contained:** `pod_combat_expire_dot_if_done_effect` removes
   the four slot vars (including `slot_dot_source`) at rounds ≤ 0. It is
   separate from the decrement so the kind flag survives until the wound
   dispatch has read it.

DoT ticks are **unsoakable by design**
(`pod_combat_apply_dot_wound_effect` mirrors PoD's wound effect minus
the soak roll); splat-weakness multiplier, damage cap, hard immunity,
and clay-man protection still apply. See **Damage pipeline** for the
wound routing.

### customizable_loc raw_text evaluates on every row

A combat-log customizable_localization renderer's `raw_text` is
evaluated on **every** log row, regardless of whether the enclosing
widget is visible — the GUI's `visible` gate suppresses *display*, not
*evaluation*. So a renderer that reads `scope:move.var:hp_damage` (or
any entry/move var) runs that read on system rows, DoT rows, and
mind-control rows that carry no such var, and an unguarded read on an
unset var errors (per the scope-trap rule above).

Every such renderer therefore opens with a **defensive first branch**
that short-circuits to a blank/neutral key when its required scope or
var is absent (`NOT = { exists = scope:move }`, `NOT = { scope:entry = {
has_variable = system_message } }`, etc.) before any var compare. The
per-segment renderers (`PODCombatLogSeg<X>`) and the result-text
dispatcher all carry this guard. When you add a
renderer or a new branch, lead with the same guard.

### Move tooltip variants

The move card (`pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip`, declared in
`pod_combat_skills_window.gui`) renders title / effects / flavor and ends
in an empty `block "additional_text" {}`. Three variants exist, and a
surface picks one by which type it names:

| Variant | Type | Surfaces |
|---|---|---|
| Base | `pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip` | combat-log rows, perk / inline-loc links |
| Eligibility | `pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip_editor` | loadout editor: equipped column, compact pool card, sheet name cell (the detailed pool card has no tooltipwidget by design and carries the same two sections inline) |
| Usability | `pod_fancy_combat_move_tooltip_live` | the four combat-bar move buttons |

Both non-base variants fill `additional_text` with failures-only reason
lines. Nothing renders when the move is usable, so the base card is what
a valid move shows in every variant.

The reason text comes from render-only scripted GUIs -
`pod_combat_move_usability_tt` and `pod_combat_loadout_block_tt` - whose
`is_valid` exists purely to be rendered by `ScriptedGui.IsValidTooltip`.
Never bind `IsValid` / `Execute` to one: they have no effect block, and
their answer is deliberately narrower than the real gate's. Each clause is

```
trigger_if = {
    limit = { <applicability>  NOT = { <shared predicate> } }
    custom_tooltip = { text = <key>  always = no }
}
```

so a passing condition emits nothing at all. `IsValidTooltip` otherwise
renders the full pass-and-fail checklist, and the shipped strings are
failure-phrased ("Select an enemy target"), which reads as nonsense on a
green line. This also matches what the editor already did for unlock
requirements (`is_valid_showing_failures_only` on the hidden decision).

Both halves of a reason - the gate that greys the button and the line
that explains it - call the SAME named predicate, so they cannot
disagree. Those live in the `SHARED GATE PREDICATES` section at the top
of `pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt`, plus the capacity and
control-slot predicates in `pod_combat_triggers.txt`. LOCKSTEP: a new
blocking gate needs a predicate, a call from the gate, and a line in the
matching `_tt` SGUI - otherwise the move greys out with no reason shown.

Cost note: both SGUI calls sit inside a `tooltipwidget` subtree, which
the engine builds only while the widget is hovered, so the documented
four-`IsValid`-per-move-button-per-frame hotspot is unaffected. The
loadout editor's `enabled` bindings do add one per-row per-frame call
(`pod_combat_loadout_room`), which is O(1) by construction - cached count
vars and list membership, never the unlock dispatcher.

### An open event keeps its scope table; trigger_event copies it inline

Two behaviours Paradox documents nowhere, both measured in-game on
2026-07-25 against a live save. The shell hand-off (§10) is built on
them, so they are recorded here rather than left to be re-derived. To
re-test either, arm a scope of each type in one event, fire a second
event from it, and read them back from the second event's option.

**A held-open event's saved-scope table survives a save and reload.**
Arm a table, leave the event on screen, save, load that save, then click
the option: characters, artifacts, titles, numeric scope values and
**flag-typed** scope values all come back, the flag still comparing
equal against its literal. That last point is the one that makes the
shell strictly more capable than the variable stash it replaced — a flag
degrades to a plain value the moment it passes through `set_variable`,
which is why `scope:method` could never be carried.

**`trigger_event` takes its copy of the table at the call site, not when
the enclosing effect finishes.** A scope value saved on the line *after*
the `trigger_event` call is not visible to the fired event. This is what
makes ordering a usable tool: firing an event early in an effect
genuinely shields it from names that effect saves later, which is why the
shell is opened at the top of the duel builder rather than after it.

Neither result should be assumed to extend to `save_temporary_scope_as`
(documented as same-effect only) or to scopes saved inside a
scripted-GUI execution, which have no chain to persist into.

---

## §8 Combat end

The normal victory check first awards a player win when no living enemy
slot remains; otherwise a KO'd PC or an empty player team loses. The
player may also end the fight through auto-resolve. All outcome paths
converge: set `pod_combat_winner` + `pod_combat_loser`, set
`pod_combat_is_over`, then the conclude affordance appears — the shell
card the duel builder opened, or the **End Combat** button for a fight
seated without it — and calls `pod_combat_end_combat_effect`.

There is deliberately no abandon-mid-fight control. The combat window's
close button is hidden (`blockoverride "button_close" { visible = no }`),
because a duel that stops half-way leaves the launching event waiting on
a hand-off that will never come. Auto-resolve is the way out of a fight
you do not want to play through.

There is a fourth, abnormal termination: the **host dying live
mid-fight** (a fatal-duel wound, or any external death). The click
path is unreachable from then on (the window binds to the dead
character's vars and events do not fire on the dead), so PoD's
`POD_on_death` (a delta in `common/on_action/POD_vanilla_death.txt`,
guarded by `has_variable = pod_combat_active`) calls
`pod_combat_host_died_release_effect`: a **dead-safe** release that
touches only living characters and stories — it strips engaged flags /
duel modifiers, clears the combat soak-tier bridge vars (else they keep
modifying `POD_soak_chance` outside the fight), clears AI scratch and
pending-reinforcement scheduling, despawns summons, and ends the
Charon-owned stories. The tier / AI-scratch / reinforcement clears are
the shared dead-safe sub-effects (`pod_combat_clear_combat_tiers_effect`,
`pod_combat_clear_all_ai_scratch_effect`,
`pod_combat_strip_pending_reinforcement_vars_effect`) that
`pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect` also calls, so the two paths cannot
drift. The dead host's own combat vars are left inert in save data
(mutating them would error; the heir is a different character). The
release is teardown-only: it ends the fight story LAST (which cascades
every slot story and every log row with it) and does not derive a duel
outcome or fire the original follow-up event. If the held shell card
survives, its no-outcome branch simply closes.
Recovering the parent duel chain after a live host death is therefore a
current limitation, not an implemented handoff.
The same release backs `.9999`'s phase 6 via an `is_alive` branch: when
phase 4's death outcome kills the losing host, phase 6 routes to the
dead-safe release instead of the full `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` (whose
character-side clears would error on the corpse).

### Victory check

`pod_combat_check_group_victory_effect` runs at every action boundary
(workspace save, end of each AI turn, after a DoT tick). It is not run
per damage event — an AoE wipe would otherwise emit one cleanup per kill
— so cleanup stays atomic. It checks three conditions in order, first
match wins:

| # | Condition | Result |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | No alive enemy slot | player wins; loser = enemy leader (enemy/0) |
| 2 | PC slot (player/0) KO'd | player loses; winner = lowest-position living enemy (enemy leader only as fallback) |
| 3 | No alive player-team slot | player loses; winner = lowest-position living enemy (covers a bypassed PC death check) |

A match sets `pod_combat_winner`, `pod_combat_loser`,
`pod_combat_is_over`, saves the victorious principal as
`scope:pod_combat_target`, and pushes the victory/defeat log row. On a
player win that scope is the defeated enemy leader; on a player loss it
is the lowest-position living enemy selected by
`pod_combat_save_enemy_victor_effect`. The combat window stays open; the
player concludes from the shell card, which appears once the fight is
decided (§10).

### Auto-resolve

One extra outcome path exists beside the win/loss check. (A concede
path used to sit here too, on the combat window's X button; the button
was removed with the shell hand-off — see §8. Its effects,
`pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect` and
`pod_combat_zero_loser_leader_morale_effect`, are still live: auto-resolve
shares them.)

**Auto-resolve** (`pod_combat_auto_resolve_effect`) skips the rest of
the fight with a prowess-sum roll. It sums each alive slot occupant's
live prowess (floored at 0 per slot, so a negative contribution can never
hand `integer_range` a negative max) per
team, adds per-team jitter (`sum + rand(0..sum)`), and the higher score
wins. Because the read is live, a team that has already taken a beating
auto-resolves from the shape it is actually in. It writes the outcome via
`set_outcome_pair_effect` (`PC_WON = yes` when the player score
ties-or-beats the enemy), zeroes the loser leader's morale, and pushes
the matching log row.

**The spirit-gate verdict runs first**, and skips the roll when it
fires. A prowess roll knows nothing about the spirit gate (§6), so
without it a character with no way of touching an incorporeal spirit
could win a fight whose every attack the gate denies - and, under
`fatality = always`, kill the spirit outright. So a side facing a living
incorporeal spirit with no damage answer to it loses instead:

* `pod_combat_slot_has_spirit_answer_trigger` (slot scope) accepts the
  init-captured `slot_can_damage_spirit` capability; an equipped ungated
  move with `hp_damage`; an equipped ungated **HP** DoT move
  (`dot_amount` is the magnitude for both kinds, so `dot_morale` must be
  excluded or morale offense smuggles itself in as damage); or an
  equipped friendly summon, which answers by proxy because every shipped
  recipe seats a wraith- or spirit-trait character that gets
  `slot_can_damage_spirit` at its own init. The summon arm sits
  **outside** the `checks_spirit_gate` test: all four summon moves carry
  that flag, inert on a self-targeting move that never reaches the
  contact resolver, and it would otherwise reject every one of them.
  It reads the **live** `pod_combat_loadout`, because AI
  loadouts are seeded *after* slot init at both finalize and mid-fight
  seating - a flag captured at init would read every AI's list as empty.
  In a duel that bars summoning (**Duel rule: summoning**) this arm is
  dropped: the summon could never be cast, so it is not an answer.
  Morale offense is deliberately excluded (see §6).
* `pod_combat_slot_has_live_hp_burn_trigger` exempts a blocker that is
  already burning down: the round-end tick keeps working after its
  caster dies (it re-attributes to the victim), so a burn is a live
  route to a KO even when nobody standing has an answer.
* **Known limitation:** the answer test does not ask whether *this*
  spirit is immune to the credited move's damage class, so e.g. a
  sun-only kit (8 ungated moves, and every incorporeal splat is
  sun-immune) still reads as an answer and falls back to the roll. The
  trigger is slot-scoped over the actor's loadout and cannot reach the
  blocking target's splat. Left deliberately: over-crediting only
  restores the old coin flip, while under-crediting would force a defeat
  on a fight the player could actually have won.
* `pod_combat_player_side_cannot_win_trigger` / `_enemy_side_..._trigger`
  (host scope) pair that with an incorporeal opponent, walking the same
  list and the same team / alive predicates as the prowess loops so the
  verdict and the roll cannot disagree about who is where. **The two are
  not mirror images**, because the win conditions in §10 are not either:
  the player must down *every* enemy (check 1), so one undamageable
  enemy left standing is enough; the enemy only needs the PC's own slot
  down (check 2 fires with allies still up), so that arm asks whether
  **the PC** is incorporeal - an incorporeal *ally* does not protect a
  corporeal PC, and reading it as though it did would hand the player a
  win the enemy could still have taken.

Each branch is cross-guarded on the other side's verdict, so the
(impossible) both-incapable case falls through to the roll rather than
forcing an outcome; it is impossible because an incorporeal combatant
passes `POD_can_naturally_damage_spirit_trigger` itself. A mid-fight
save predating the spirit slot flags carries them on no slot, so the
incorporeal arm reads false and auto-resolve rolls exactly as it used
to - the fail-open direction throughout, since over-crediting an answer
only costs a forced verdict, never a wrong one.

The button stays **enabled** under a forced loss on purpose: with no
flee or concede path, auto-resolve is the only exit from a fight the
gate has made unwinnable, so clicking it concedes. The tooltip says so -
`pod_combat_auto_resolve_desc` appends `PODCombatAutoResolveVerdict`,
which emits a warning line on a forced loss, a positive line on a forced
win, and the blank key otherwise.

Both paths write the winner/loser pair atomically through
`pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect`, which always sets **both** vars on
**both** branches — the post-combat consumer gates on both, so a
half-set pair would silently downgrade a decisive result to a draw. On a
PC win the loser is the enemy leader; on an enemy win the winner is
recorded by `pod_combat_save_enemy_victor_effect`, which records the
lowest-position alive enemy slot's char (falling back to the enemy
leader only when every enemy is KO'd), so a downed leader is never
recorded as the victor.

### Ending the combat

`pod_combat_end_combat_effect` is re-entry guarded on the FIGHT's
`pod_combat_active` - it asks the fight whether it is live, not whether
the character still points at one, because the SC route keeps the pointer
after clearing active (the GUI execute and End Combat both call it). It:

1. **Health finalization + death-gate strip**
   (`pod_combat_finalize_combat_health_effect`): for `fatality = no`, caps
   each combatant's wound track to keep at least one empty box (peeling a
   superficial wound first, else an aggravated one); for `practice`,
   restores each combatant's wound track to the snapshot taken at finalize
   (`pod_combat_restore_practice_wounds_effect`) so the spar leaves no
   lasting harm; the fatal modes keep their wounds untouched. Every mode
   then strips the `pod_combat_nonfatal_active` death-gate flag — for the
   capped mode in that order, so the inline death check can't fire during
   the pass. Nothing dies here: the caller reaches `pod_combat.9999`
   through `trigger_event`, which silently no-ops on a dead host, so a kill
   at this site would lose the follow-up and the teardown. The fatal modes'
   deaths are settled at the writeback instead (**Deferred death
   resolution**).
2. **Morale → Mental Strain** (`pod_combat_apply_morale_strain_effect`,
   skipped entirely for `practice` spars): one batched pass over alive
   combatants. Each takes
   `POD_static_stress_gain` from its final morale deficit —
   `(pod_combat_slot_max_morale_value − slot_morale) ×
   pod_combat_morale_stress_multiplier` (multiplier = 5). Batched to one
   pass because `POD_static_stress_gain` is heavy (per-call skill
   recalc).
3. Saves winner/loser as named scopes and runs
   `pod_combat_duel_end_effect`, which strips each slot's discipline
   battle modifiers and the `pod_combat_duel_active` marker while slot
   vars are still intact.
4. Branches on whether this was a duel-driven combat. If
   `pod_combat_session` exists, it removes the fight's
   `pod_combat_active` (keeping `pod_combat_is_over` as a re-entry
   guard) and fires `pod_combat.9999`. Otherwise (standalone/debug
   combat) it shows a victory/defeat toast and runs
   `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` immediately.

Who *calls* it decides how much context survives — see the next section.

### Post-combat handoff

A duel-launched fight has to hand back to the event chain that started
it, and that chain's saved scopes are the problem: the combat window's
buttons are scripted-GUI executions, which carry no scope table, so
anything reached from a button click arrives with nothing.

The shell event solves it by never leaving the chain (§7 records the two
engine behaviours this rests on, both measured rather than assumed).
`POD_combat_create_duel_effect` fires `pod_combat.9995` with no delay as
its opening block, so every fight the duel builder starts — PoD's
single-combat launcher and direct builder calls alike — inherits its
caller's entire table: the `sc_*` principals, the fatality / fixed /
locale rule flags, and every alias the parent named for its own
follow-up, flag-typed scope values included. That event stays open for
the whole fight, which is what keeps the table alive. Its **Conclude** option then runs the writeback, so
`pod_combat.9999` fires from inside the original chain and the follow-up
reads the parent's scopes exactly as an uninterrupted vanilla duel would
have delivered them.

The *window* (`gui/event_windows/pod_combat_shell.gui`) is a separate
matter from the event. It is withheld while the fight runs and appears
centred once the fight is decided, so nothing overlaps the combat window
during play. Note the asymmetry: the event has to open at launch, since
being fired from inside the launching effect is what captures the scope
table, and an event opened at the end would be fired from a scope-less
GUI click. Only the presentation is deferred.

**Ordering inside the builder matters.** The card is fired before
anything else in `POD_combat_create_duel_effect`, because the builder's
helpers save generic names into the same chain
(`pod_combat_calc_turn_orders_effect` writes `scope:host` and
`scope:me`), which would otherwise shadow same-named scopes belonging to
the parent — an activity duel's `scope:host` is the activity host, and
silently substituting the combat host is worse than a missing scope.

The one thing that runs *ahead* of the card is the builder's own save of
the canonical duel names from its parameters: `sc_initiator` /
`sc_attacker` / `sc_defender` and the `fatality` / `fixed` / `locale`
flags. The card's conclude gate and `.9999`'s live path read the
principals off the inherited table, and finalization reads
`scope:fatality`, so the card has to carry them. The single-combat
launcher already saved those exact names and values, making the re-save a
no-op there, while a direct builder call gets a self-sufficient card
instead of the degraded rebuild. The `pod_combat_sc_shell` marker is set
last, after the fight stands up, because the builder's own stale-fight
teardown would clear it.

`pod_combat.9999` then runs:

1. Bind `scope:sc_ctx` from the host's `pod_combat_session` pointer -
   `sc_ctx` **is** the fight story now, not a separate context story.
   The deref is `?=`-guarded, since an unset or already-ended pointer
   would error and take the whole dispatch down. Both routes need it;
   the fight carries the follow-up id.
2. **Fallback, only when `scope:sc_attacker` is absent.** That means the
   dispatcher was reached from a GUI execution rather than the shell, so
   the principals and rule flags are rebuilt from the fight story
   (`POD_combat_restore_sc_context_effect` +
   `POD_combat_restore_sc_rule_flags_effect`). It also fabricates the
   vanilla-compatible attacker aliases `attacker` / `challenger` and
   defender aliases `victim` / `recipient` / `defender` / `target` /
   `adversary`. Parent-specific aliases are unrecoverable on this route,
   so the follow-up still runs degraded. Nothing routes here while every
   fight goes through `POD_combat_create_duel_effect`, which opens the
   card; it stands as the defensive arm for a fight seated without the
   builder. A card-carrying fight reaching it lost a table it did have,
   so that case `error_log`s (gated on `pod_combat_sc_shell`).
3. **Derive the outcome** (`POD_combat_derive_sc_outcome_effect`), the
   same on both routes since it reads combat-owned fight vars: `sc_victor`
   / `sc_loser` plus the victor snap, and the `sc_finished` /
   `victory_type` constants. It runs after the fallback, whose principals
   the snap compares against.
4. **Apply duel finalization side effects**
   (`POD_combat_apply_duel_finalization_effect`): release both
   principals' `engaged_in_single_combat` state; increment one equipped
   primary weapon's `artifact_duel_var` for the victor; grant the victor
   Fera ferocity renown outside practice; then apply fatality-mode death
   handling. Two stages, in this order: the guaranteed-kill modes
   (`fatality = always`, and `default` on tribal governments) force
   `pod_death` on the loser regardless of wound math, guarded by
   `is_alive`; then `pod_combat_resolve_suppressed_deaths_effect` settles
   every death the in-fight gate deferred (**Deferred death resolution**).
   `practice` and `no` apply no death at all.
5. Fire the follow-up event (`OUTPUT_EVENT`), with an initiator →
   defender → attacker fallback chain matching vanilla.
6. **Cleanup** (`pod_combat_cleanup_effect`): clears the character-side
   combat soak tiers and the weapon profile, runs
   `pod_combat_group_cleanup_effect` (drops pending reinforcements,
   clears AI scratch, despawns summons), then `end_story`s the **fight**
   story and drops the `pod_combat_session` pointer. The fight goes LAST
   on every teardown path: everything above may still read it, nothing
   may after, and ending it is what fires the cascade. That one
   `end_story` tears down every slot story and every log row and drops
   the whole fight state atomically - workspace mirrors, counters,
   outcome, `pod_combat_step_mode`, the `sc_*` principals, the rule flags
   and the follow-up id - which is why nothing above needs a matching
   `remove_variable`, and why adding one is a mistake. The hand-written
   slot / log teardown loops were deleted when the cascade landed;
   re-adding one would end those stories twice.
   (`pod_combat_turn_order_list` is deliberately NOT cascaded - it is a
   reordered view of the same slot stories.)

#### Deferred death resolution

The minigame writes real wounds live on every hit, so PoD's inline death
check (`POD_health_death_check_effect`, reached through
`POD_apply_wound_boxes`) would otherwise fire *mid-action*. That is
unusable: for a victim with guaranteed resurrection — wraith, spectre,
hsien, siochain, `extra_life_*`, the phoenix / scarab / rebirthing perks —
`pod_death`'s death arm is inadmissible, so the only branch left is
`pod_resurrection_effect` → `POD_full_heal_effect`, a **free full heal**
mid-fight. The minigame's kill test is `is_alive`, so it saw a living
full-HP enemy, clamped both wound deltas to 0, logged the killing blow as
*Soaked*, and the fight could never end. Umbra duels surfaced it because
their opponent pool is mostly wraiths and spectres.

So every fight now carries the death gate for its whole duration
(`pod_combat_uses_death_gate_trigger`, which no longer looks at the
fatality mode), a lethally-wounded combatant is **KO'd** rather than
killed — the KO predicate's `POD_total_damage_boxes >=
POD_health_track_length` clause already covered exactly this — and the
deferred deaths resolve once at the writeback, after
`pod_combat_finalize_combat_health_effect` has stripped the gate. This is
the model base PoD already uses: `work_out_wounds_or_death_effect`
(`events/single_combat_events.txt`) applies duel wounds and death exactly
once, at the end.

`pod_combat_resolve_suppressed_deaths_effect` (last phase of
`POD_combat_apply_duel_finalization_effect`) walks
`pod_combat_active_slots` off `scope:sc_ctx` — a story, so it cannot
become a corpse part-way through the loop — and calls PoD's own
`POD_health_death_check_effect` once per surviving combatant. Reusing
PoD's check rather than a bare `pod_death` is what preserves the rest of
the contract: it re-tests track-full **and** aggravated ≥
`POD_aggravated_damage_threshold_for_death` **and**
`POD_can_die_from_wounds`, so a combatant beaten down on superficial
damage alone walks away, and an immortal *without* guaranteed
resurrection still takes the `POD_long_depose_effect` off-ramp instead of
dying.

Three rules the sweep keeps:

* **Bloodless modes are skipped** (`pod_combat_duel_is_bloodless_trigger`
  — `fatality = no` and `practice`). That trigger holds the *old* body of
  the death gate, and is also what the CERTÁMEN duel title asks, so a
  to-the-death mage duel still titles itself DUEL.
* **Summons are skipped** (`pod_combat_slot_char_is_summon_trigger`, read
  off the fight's `pod_combat_summoned_chars` registry). Cleanup removes
  them with `death_disappearance`; killing one would leave the corpse the
  fight is supposed to erase.
* **Killer = the opposing side's principal**, captured into
  `scope:pod_combat_player_principal` / `scope:pod_combat_enemy_victor` at
  the *top* of the finalization effect — before any forced kill, since
  both savers read the fight through the host and the host is exactly who
  a `fatality = always` loss turns into a corpse. A victim whose opposite
  number is unset or already dead is left standing rather than handed to
  `POD_commit_murder_effect` with a dead killer; PoD settles their full
  track at the next `POD_refresh_health_track`.

A wraith killed at the writeback still resurrects once. That is correct
and is what base PoD does — the duel has already resolved — and the
spectre it becomes is erased the next day by `POD_umbra_lost.100`.

**The card is the only way to conclude a fight the builder started.** The
combat window's **End Combat** button hides itself whenever the marker is
set (`Not(PODCombatFight.Var('pod_combat_sc_shell').IsSet)`) — which every
builder-started fight now is, leaving that button as the escape hatch for
a fight seated without the builder — and the window has no close button
at all. Two
reasons, and the second is the subtle one: concluding from a GUI click
would drop the scopes, *and* it would leave the card open with nobody to
dismiss it — a card is only dismissed by selecting its option. An
orphaned card re-enables during the next fight, which is why the option
is guarded by
`pod_combat_shell_owns_live_fight_trigger` rather than by
`has_variable = pod_combat_active` alone, and why the card stays
clickable (as a no-op that closes it) whenever no fight is live.

