## §10 Parent-scope hand-off

The events that call `configure_start_single_combat_effect` routinely
save custom scopes — `scope:spirit`, `scope:my_rival`, `scope:scheme`,
an artifact `scope:target`, `scope:task_contract` — that their follow-up
reads once the duel resolves, often one or two events further
downstream. Under vanilla's duel chain those scopes simply propagate
through the event graph.

The minigame breaks that graph: its buttons are scripted-GUI executions
with no scope table, so anything reached from a click starts empty.

### How it works now

`pod_combat.9995`, the shell event, is fired with no delay from
`POD_combat_create_duel_effect` (from its opening block, so every builder
caller gets it) and stays open for the whole fight. Firing it from inside
the launching event's effect means it
inherits that event's entire scope table, and holding it open is what
keeps the table alive while the fight runs in a window whose clicks
cannot carry one. Concluding from its option puts `pod_combat.9999` back
inside the original chain, so the follow-up — and everything downstream
of it — sees the parent's scopes natively.

Its window is hidden until the fight resolves (§8), which is a
presentation choice only. The event's open-from-launch lifetime is not
negotiable: an event opened at the end of the fight would be fired from
a scripted-GUI click and would inherit nothing.

Consequences worth knowing:

- **Nothing is enumerated.** New PoD duel content that saves new scopes
  works with no combat-side change. There is no whitelist to audit on a
  PoD update.
- **Flag-typed scope values ride along.** They could never survive the
  old variable copy (flags degrade to a plain value through
  `set_variable`), which is why `scope:method` was permanently
  unsupported.
- **No fabricated aliases on the live path.** The old restore invented
  `scope:target` / `scope:victim` / `scope:recipient` from the
  principals, which silently clobbered a parent's real ones — that is
  how an artifact duel's prize went missing, the `target` scope being an
  artifact rather than a character. The parent's own values now arrive
  intact, so the whole bug class is structurally gone.
- **Rule flags need no round trip.** `fatality` / `fixed` / `locale` are
  scope values on the inherited table (the parent's own, or the ones the
  builder seeds from its parameters just before firing the card), so the
  enum ladders that rebuilt them from story variables only run on the
  fallback route. The `summons` rule is deliberately in neither place:
  nothing after the fight reads it, so it is stamped on the fight story
  and never round-tripped as a scope value.

The order inside the builder is load-bearing: the card fires **before**
the rest of `POD_combat_create_duel_effect`, because the builder's helpers
save generic names (`scope:host`, `scope:me`) into the same chain and
would shadow same-named parent scopes. §8 has that plus the
card-ownership rules —
a card outlives its fight if the host dies mid-fight, so the option is
gated on `pod_combat_shell_owns_live_fight_trigger` rather than on
"a fight is running".

### The fallback route

`pod_combat.9999` detects a missing `scope:sc_attacker` and rebuilds the
principals and rule flags from the fight story (`POD_combat_fight`). Parent
aliases cannot be recovered. The restore therefore also fabricates the
standard defender aliases (`victim`, `recipient`, `defender`, `target`,
`adversary`) and attacker aliases (`attacker`, `challenger`) from the
principals. A follow-up needing a parent-specific alias still runs
degraded. Nothing routes here in practice:

| Route | Treatment |
|---|---|
| A fight seated without `POD_combat_create_duel_effect` | The route this arm exists for. No card, no marker, concluded from the combat window's button. No such caller exists today. |
| A card-carrying fight | Should be unreachable — the card is its only conclude path. `error_log`s if it ever happens. |

The story therefore still carries the principals, the rule flags and
the follow-up id. Live host death currently ends that story during the
dead-safe release without firing the follow-up; no dead-host bridge is
implemented.

### First-fight intro deferral

On a character's first minigame fight the launch is deferred behind an
intro popup, which adds one hop to the chain above:

```
caller's option (table T)
  -> pod_combat.0200                     (intro; inherits T, holds it open)
     -> [optional] pod_combat.0201       (ready bar; inherits T, editor opens under it)
        -> Begin option -> the builder -> pod_combat.9995 (snapshots T)
```

`pod_combat_sc_intro_or_launch_effect` (in
`PoD_override_single_combat_effects.txt`) fires the intro INSTEAD of
calling the launcher, because `trigger_event` only queues: a popup fired
beside the launch would open on a fight that had already started, with
the loadout editor hidden by then. `pod_combat_intro_begin_fight_effect`
(`pod_combat_intro_effects.txt`) runs the builder from the option, which
is what keeps the card's snapshot equal to the direct route's. The
launch can never move to the editor's close button - that is a
scripted-GUI execution and carries no table at all.

Rules the two intro events live by, for the same reason the shell does:
**no `trigger` block** (a failing trigger would swallow the deferral and
strand `engaged_in_single_combat` on both principals) and **no scope
saves** beyond the deliberately namespaced `scope:pod_combat_intro_opponent`
(anything else would ride T into the follow-up and could shadow a parent
alias).

Three accepted consequences, all once per character:

- **`follow_up` is restored, not passed.** Macro args take literal
  tokens, so the deferred launch hands the builder the placeholder
  `pod_combat.0299` and writes the real id onto the fight story straight
  after (`var:pod_combat_session ?= { set_variable = { name = follow_up
  value = scope:follow_up_event } }`). This is load-bearing:
  `pod_combat.9999` fires the follow-up from that var on **every** route.
  If the write is ever lost, `pod_combat.0299` error-logs instead of a
  wrong follow-up firing silently.
- **`scope:locale` is `flag:battlefield` on the card's table.** Same
  literal-arg problem, but here the builder's own re-save clobbers the
  inherited value *before* the card snapshot, so no post-launch fixup can
  reach it. Verified reader-free: on the minigame route nothing reads
  `scope:locale` after `create_duel` (the only readers are the vanilla
  Part-4 chain events and the self-contained `game_rule` 1066 chain), and
  the story var is consumed only on the degraded fallback path, where
  `battlefield` is already the restore ladder's default. Re-check this if
  a follow-up ever starts reading `scope:locale`.
- **Post-launch decoration no-ops.** `POD_combat_set_custom_title` and
  the team-name setters are `?=`-guarded on the duel story, which does
  not exist yet on the deferred branch, so a first fight from the heist,
  umbra or debug launchers runs with the default window title. This is
  why the summoning rule opts in *before* the launch instead: a rule that
  quietly lapsed on a character's first fight would be a defect, not a
  cosmetic loss.

`fatality` is *not* in that list: it reaches the builder through a
five-arm literal ladder matching
`POD_combat_restore_sc_rule_flags_effect`, so the story var is correct
and the death gate and practice snapshot behave exactly as on the direct
route. Keep the two ladders in lockstep - a `FATALITY` literal present
there and missing here silently becomes a duel to the death.

While the intro is pending, the Part-3 gate refuses to intercept further
duels for either principal (`pod_combat_intro_pending`), so a second duel
runs as a vanilla one rather than starting a minigame fight underneath
the open card. That vanilla duel releases `engaged_in_single_combat` on
its way out, which is why Begin re-stamps it and why
`pod_combat_intro_launch_valid_trigger` deliberately does not test it.

### Victor snapping

`pod_combat_set_outcome_pair_effect` can record a SUMMON as the
winner (PC lost; the first alive enemy was a summoned combatant).
Vanilla duels only ever name the two duelists, and follow-up identity
compares (`sc_victor = scope:recidivist`, `= scope:spirit`, ...)
assume that — so `POD_combat_derive_sc_outcome_effect` snaps a
non-principal victor to the winning side's principal (the loser is
always a principal, so the victor is whichever principal the loser
isn't). The snapped principal can be dead; follow-ups already tolerate
dead victors. This fixed the diablerize / vamphunt / umbra-crisis
chains in one place. It runs after the fallback restore, so the
principals it compares against exist on both routes.

Repurposed vanilla outputs are unaffected: the two PoD launchers that
pointed at `tribal.1021` (the vanilla liege-challenge output, which
reads liege-chain scopes they never set and docks the surviving victor a
prestige level on an attacker death) still point at the dedicated
`pod_combat.9990` aftermath event instead.
---

## §11 Extending the system

Short pointers for the common extension tasks. Each cross-references the
section that describes the system being extended; the multi-file fan-out
cases (a new outcome flag, a new damage class, a new combat-log
segment, a new capability flag, a new marker character modifier) are in
**Cross-file change checklists** below.

### Add an execution phase

Pick a numeric position in `POD_execute_combat_move`
(`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`), respecting the ordering invariants in
**Move execution** (damage before its rider gates; log push after every
outcome-producing phase and before teardown).
Document the new phase's dependencies in the phase table. If the phase
produces a new `pod_combat_last_*` flag or amount, add it to the result
reset block (§2) and to `pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect` so it
can't leak between moves.

### Add a reaction class

A reaction is a (condition, payload) pair armed in the retaliation-setup
phase and fired in the reaction phase. To add one (see **Reactions**):

1. Add the workspace slots (amount + kind flag + morale companion) and
   mirror them on both sides of the load/save helpers (see the
   workspace-mirror task below), plus a setup branch that writes them.
2. Pick the result-code partition trigger that matches the desired
   condition (`pod_combat_result_code_indicates_*`), or add a new one.
3. Add a `pod_combat_apply_reaction_if_triggered_effect` call in
   `pod_combat_check_retaliation_effect` with that `CONDITION_TRIGGER`.

The shared payload helper handles the spirit gate, the bypass-guard
damage call, the retal-damage lift, and the
`pod_combat_last_retal_fired` overlay, so a new reaction is a few lines
of declaration. The recipe also lives in the reaction-phase header of
`pod_combat_effects.txt`.

### Retune tier magnitudes or add a tier stat category

Edit `pod_combat_tier_effects.txt` directly only when the **stat
categories** change (currently `soak` and `hit`). The shipped
±10/20/30 runtime magnitudes are single-sourced as the
`pod_combat_tier_small_mag` / `pod_combat_tier_medium_mag` /
`pod_combat_tier_large_mag` script values in `pod_combat_values.txt`, read
by name everywhere they apply (the tier recalc, the GUI display SVs, and
`POD_soak_chance` in PoD's `POD_health_track_values.txt`). Retuning
runtime behavior therefore starts with those three SV defaults (or the
debug overrides), because move setters carry ordinal 1/2/3 rather than
the magnitude. Also update the hard-coded 10/20/30 tutorial
localization, the tier tables in this reference/authoring guide, and AI
comments that describe the shipped curve.

This is not a fourth-tier checklist. Adding a new ordinal would require
new magnitude and dispatch branches across the hit recalc, soak bridge,
move/slot display values, GUI/custom-loc presentation, setters,
validation, AI scoring, and documentation. Treat it as a cross-system
feature rather than editing only the three existing SVs. See **Tier
system**.

### Add a workspace-mirror field

Any combatant field that must be readable across both the actor and target phases of an action lives as a mirror pair (`pod_combat_actor_X` / `pod_combat_target_X`) on the fight story. Because load/save are parametric over `SIDE = actor|target`, a new mirror is one line per helper, not one per side:

1. Declare the slot field (a `pod_combat_init_slot_*` writer or a
   move-execution writer); document it in **Variable reference**.
2. Add the slot→workspace copy to
   `pod_combat_load_side_from_story_helper`
   (`pod_combat_group_effects.txt`).
3. Add the workspace→slot writeback to
   `pod_combat_save_side_to_story_helper` (same file).
4. Audit direct slot writes. If the new `slot_*` field can be written
   while that slot is the loaded actor or target, mirror the write into
   the matching workspace field immediately; otherwise the next save
   can overwrite it with the stale cached value. See
   `pod_combat_apply_ally_down_shock_to_slot_effect` for the pattern.

There is deliberately **no cleanup step**. The workspace is fight-owned,
so it dies with the fight story's `end_story`; adding a `remove_variable`
for it to `pod_combat_cleanup_effect` is dead code.

The load and save field lists must stay in lockstep — a field added to
one but not the other desyncs at every action boundary. If the var is
set parametrically rather than by a literal `set_variable`, add the
matching error-suppression entry.

### Add a per-character initiative or starting-morale modifier

Two pre-wired designer hooks let a developer hand any character a flat
bonus or penalty to combat initiative, or to the morale they start a
fight with, without touching the engine. Both are script values in
`pod_combat_values.txt`
(`pod_combat_initiative_modifier_value` /
`pod_combat_starting_morale_modifier_value`); both return `0` by
default, and both are already summed into their canonical value:
the per-round roll in `pod_combat_calculate_initiative_effect`, and
`pod_combat_starting_morale_value`, which both slot-seeding sites read.
To use one, fill in its body; nothing else needs wiring.

Each is evaluated on the combatant's **slot** story (`this` = the slot), with the occupant at `var:slot_char`. Branch on the character through it - `var:slot_char` scope-switches are valid inside a script_value (engine precedent: `04_ep2_accolade_values.txt`):

```
pod_combat_initiative_modifier_value = {
    value = 0
    if = { limit = { var:slot_char = { has_trait = brave } } add = 5 }
}
```

- Keep the body a **pure read** - conditions plus `add`/`subtract`, no effects.
- **Initiative** is re-evaluated every round, so it may read live fight
  state. **Starting morale** is evaluated once for the constructor's
  provisional alive seed and again during per-slot fight initialization;
  the second seed is the combatant's actual starting morale. Both sites
  call `pod_combat_starting_morale_value`, and both run after `slot_char`
  is written.
- Morale is per-combatant, and so is its cap. Its baseline is
  `pod_combat_slot_max_morale_value` (or the debug-tuned starting-morale
  override), plus this hook. A positive modifier may push the seed above that
  baseline (intended head-room); it is floored at 1, so an over-negative
  value can never start a fighter already KO'd.

#### The morale cap is scope-dependent

The cap scales off the same PoD wound track that supplies max HP, so there is
no single "the cap" value to read. `pod_combat_max_morale_base` is the
**baseline only** - what a 6-box combatant gets (20, or the debug-tuned
override). Each wound box above or below 6 moves that combatant's own ceiling
by 5. Comparing a combatant's morale against the base directly is a bug; read
the wrapper matching your scope:

| Scope | Read |
|---|---|
| character | `pod_combat_char_max_morale_value` |
| slot story | `pod_combat_slot_max_morale_value` |
| combat host, acting side | `pod_combat_actor_max_morale_value` |
| combat host, target side | `pod_combat_target_max_morale_value` |

The last two stay **character**-anchored on purpose - the GUI calls them
as `GetPlayer.MakeScope.ScriptValue` - and reach the fight-owned workspace
through `pod_combat_session`. That column is the anchor scope, not the
owner.

Each wrapper falls back to the bare baseline when its character pointer is
absent (the tooltip-build case). Because `POD_health_track_base_length` is a
splat ladder, the resulting caps are mortal 10 / ghoul, mummy, wraith 15 /
vampire, kuei-jin, risen 20 / fera, demon, fae 25 / werewolf 30, with the
per-character track perks (Shintai of Bone, Age of Kings, ...) riding on top.
Every splat trait the ladder does not name - revenant, kinfolk, laham,
nephilim, mage, changeling, zombu - falls to its 4-box `else` arm and so caps
at 10 alongside plain mortals.

### Add a move

Add the constructor and setter block, shared unlock rule and dispatcher
leaf, hidden decision, three localization keys, and version increment
directly in their live files. See the Combat Move Authoring Guide
(in-tree mirror `COMBAT_MOVE_AUTHORING_GUIDE.info`) for copyable
templates, the complete setter contract, balance rubric, verification
checklist, and test plan.

### Cross-file change checklists

The recipes above are contained to one or two files. Some features fan
out across many files instead, where a partial change compiles fine but
silently produces the wrong runtime behaviour (a new damage class shows
as a broken loc key; a new outcome flag renders blank in the log). These
checklists are the antidote: one grep'able list of "if you touch X, also
touch Y."

#### Adding a new damage class

E.g. acid, void. The single-axis classification (`var:damage_type`, see
**Damage pipeline**) fans out across damage routing, PoD wound-system
integration, AI weakness selection, tooltip rendering, and the
combat-log dispatcher.

1. **Move authoring**: once engine support exists, a move calls
   `POD_combat_move_set_damage_class = { VALUE = X }` directly, always
   **after** `POD_combat_move_set_hp_damage`, whose default
   `superficial` stamp would clobber an earlier class write. Add the
   literal class flag to validator suppression with the rest of the
   engine change.
2. **The five kind switches** — add one `flag:X` → `REASON` arm to
   each, kept in step. Three live under the "HP DAMAGE DISPATCH" banner
   in `pod_combat_move_effects.txt`
   (`pod_combat_apply_single_hp_damage_effect`: the `change_class`
   conversion, the weapon retype, and the authored class), plus
   `pod_combat_deal_typed_retal_damage_effect` (typed counters,
   `pod_combat_effects.txt`) and
   `pod_combat_deal_dot_tick_from_host_effect` (DoT ticks, same file).
3. **Kind seeding**: `pod_combat_seed_kind_from_class_effect` and
   `pod_combat_override_kind_from_bonus_class_effect`
   (`pod_combat_move_effects.txt`), plus
   `pod_combat_read_weapon_class_effect` /
   `pod_combat_override_kind_from_weapon_effect`
   (`pod_combat_weapon_effects.txt`) — add the literal-flag branch to
   each so counters and DoTs can carry the class however it was sourced.
   The two waste triggers in `pod_combat_weapon_triggers.txt`
   (`pod_combat_weapon_retype_is_wasted_trigger`,
   `pod_combat_stamped_type_is_wasted_trigger`) need the matching
   immunity arm, or a transient-source kind of the new class will never
   stand down against an immune victim. If the class should be
   authorable as a `change_class` conversion, add it to the generator's
   `DAMAGE_ELEMENTS` too.
4. **Class partition triggers**: `pod_combat_triggers.txt` — add the
   class to `pod_combat_move_class_is_elemental_trigger`, and to
   `pod_combat_class_always_aggravated_trigger` only if it belongs in
   that **typical-target AI/log heuristic**. That trigger is not
   universal truth: it includes fire and balefire despite incorporeal
   exceptions. Per-victim truth stays in PoD's classifier.
5. **PoD classification and immunity**:
   `common/scripted_triggers/POD_health_track_triggers.txt` — add the
   class's per-splat branches to `POD_damage_type_is_aggravated` and
   `POD_has_immunity_to_damage_type`, including any new authoritative
   weakness trigger.
6. **PoD multiplier and cap**:
   `common/script_values/POD_health_track_values.txt` — add any
   `POD_wound_multiplier_from_splat_weaknesses` branch and audit the
   damage-cap routing. A class can classify correctly yet still miss its
   intended doubled/capped box behavior.
7. **AI weakness mapping**:
   `pod_combat_damage_source_weakness_triggers.txt`
   — if the class is a target-specific weakness, add a per-class
   trigger + an arm in the bridge trigger. The AI uses these
   for weakness-bias move selection.
8. **Death reason definition**: if step 2 introduces a new
   `death_<reason>` instead of reusing an existing one, define it in
   `common/deathreasons/00_PoD_deaths.txt` and add the matching
   localization in every maintained language.
9. **Move-card and combat presentation**:
   `pod_combat_skill_guis.txt` — extend `pod_combat_move_desc` (drain /
   no-drain branches) and the retaliation/damage-class presentation
   switches. In `POD_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`, audit
   `PODCombatLogSegRetalArmed`, `PODCombatMoveDoTType`,
   `PODCombatSheetType`, `PODCombatCLSource`, and the
   `PODCombatSuscAgg_*` / `PODCombatSuscImm_*` portrait-popup segments,
   plus a `[subject.Custom(...)]` hop appended to BOTH
   `pod_combat_susc_agg_row` and `pod_combat_susc_imm_row`. These surfaces
   are independent; a class can deal correct wounds while showing blank or
   misleading compact/sheet/status text.
10. **Static loc**: `pod_combat_static_l_english.yml` — add 2 new
   loc keys: `pod_combat_desc_hp_damage_X` and
   `pod_combat_desc_hp_damage_X_drain`, inlining the element's colored
   label. Do **not** add a bare flag-name key (`fire`, `silver`, …
   collide with vanilla loc keys) — only `superficial`/`aggravated` ever
   reach `GetFlagName` (the `_basic` keys), because the SGUI dispatch
   sends every elemental move to its per-element key.
11. **Game concept**: the combat concept deltas in PoD's
   `00_POD_game_concepts.txt` — add a concept entry so the class
   name is hover-able as a concept tooltip.
12. **Error suppression**: `pod_combat_error_suppression_effects.txt`
    — add `pod_combat_suppress_flag = { FLAG = X }`. Required because
    the flag is set via macro substitution and read via customizable
    localization (both validator-invisible).
13. **Damage icon**: optionally add
    `gfx/interface/icons/combat/X_damage.dds` plus the matching
    texticon in `POD_shared/pod_texticons_combat.gui`
    if visual styling is desired.
14. **DoT path** (if the class can appear on a `dot_amount` move): the
    tick's kind → REASON switch is already covered by step 2. Add the
    class to the move-description and active-status type dispatchers so
    players can see the authored source class. The application row stays
    HP-class-neutral because the future victim is not known there; each
    resolved tick uses PoD's actual per-victim aggravated classifier.

#### Adding a combat-log segment

The append-only recipe:

1. **Bake block** in `pod_combat_push_log_effect`
   (`pod_combat_effects.txt`): copy an existing segment block into
   the visibility bake at the new segment's display position.
   Condition — intent segments: move-var check via
   `var:move = { ... }` + the shared burst-gate trigger; outcome
   segments: entry-amount check + outcome suppression. Then stamp
   `seg_X_on` / `seg_X_sep` and bump `seg_bake_prior`. Appending
   touches nothing else; inserting mid-order just places the block
   earlier.
2. **Thin SVs** in `pod_combat_values.txt` (entry scope; copy an
   existing pair): `pod_combat_log_seg_X_visible_value` reads
   `seg_X_on`, `pod_combat_log_before_X_value` reads `seg_X_sep`.
3. **Renderer** `PODCombatLogSegX` in `POD_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`
   — defensive `NOT exists scope:move` first branch, then this
   segment's branches only, blank fallback last. A renderer reached
   through `PODSegText` runs in a **nested** customizable-loc context.
   A nested dispatcher cannot read the OUTER saved scopes from inside
   its own triggers: it sees no `scope:entry` / `scope:move` and falls
   through to its fallback. What it can do is be **re-rooted on a saved
   scope** - `[move.Custom('SomeDispatcher')]` runs `SomeDispatcher`
   with the move story as its root, which is the standard engine idiom
   (vanilla uses it heavily, e.g. `[second.Custom('quirk_pos_noun')]`).
   So a shared dispatcher is fine as long as it reads its own root
   rather than a saved scope. `pod_combat_log_seg_marker` is the worked
   example: one key-agnostic line that re-roots
   `PODCombatAppliedMarkerName` onto `move` for the marker's name.
   To reuse a display name without any dispatcher, interpolate the
   other key with `$other_loc_key$`.
4. **Widget pair** in `pod_combat_window.gui` using
   `PODSegSepVisible` / `PODSegVisible` / `PODSegText`, mounted at the
   **same position the bake block occupies** — separators are decided
   at bake time from the count of preceding segments, so order must
   match, and a missing pair means the segment silently never renders.
5. **Loc fragment key(s)** in `POD_combat_static_l_english.yml`.
6. **Error suppression** for any NEW entry var or message flag the
   renderer reads (`pod_combat_error_suppression_effects.txt`).
7. **Outcome segments only**: lift the amount to a
   `pod_combat_last_*` fight var in the pipeline, capture it in
   `push_log`, and add it to the shared
   `pod_combat_clear_per_target_state_effect` list (which
   `pod_combat_clear_last_result_vars_effect` delegates to) so it
   cannot leak onto a later row. If the lift happens **after** the
   per-target AoE pushes, as `ap_refund` does at §14, also add the var
   to the §16 wrap-up gate, or an AoE cast of the move logs nothing.

#### Adding a new outcome flag

E.g. a new `pod_combat_last_outcome` value beyond the current
`attack` / `heal` / `morale_damage` / `morale_healed` / `utility`.
The 3-axis result model is documented in §4 above; this checklist
covers the cross-file impact of expanding the outcome axis.

1. **Set the flag**: pick the writer site. Existing writers live in
   `pod_combat_effects.txt` (e.g.
   `pod_combat_set_outcome_if_unset_effect`).
2. **Customizable loc dispatcher**: `pod_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`
   — `PODCombatLogResultText` adds a new branch on
   `scope:entry.var:outcome = flag:X`.
3. **Loc key**: `pod_combat_static_l_english.yml` — add the new
   `pod_combat_log_result_text_X` (and any sub-keys if branching
   further on attack_result).
4. **Partition triggers**: `pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt` —
   any partition trigger that should include / exclude the new
   outcome (e.g. `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_contact_trigger`,
   `pod_combat_result_code_indicates_hp_damage_trigger`). Audit each.
5. **Rider gate**: `pod_combat_rider_condition_passes_trigger` in
   `pod_combat_move_execution_triggers.txt` — if the outcome is
   rider-eligible (carries chained damage / heal / morale riders),
   add a branch to the rider condition dispatcher.
6. **Damage payload reader**:
   `pod_combat_apply_damage_payload_effect` may need an outcome-aware
   branch if the new flag should change payload-application logic.
7. **Error suppression**: `pod_combat_error_suppression_effects.txt`
   — `pod_combat_suppress_flag = { FLAG = X }`.
8. **Documentation**: update §4 result codes table in this file with
   the new flag's meaning and the partition triggers that include
   it.

#### Adding a new capability flag

E.g. `checks_X`, `requires_X` on the move story — capability flags
gate behavior in the contact resolver and related pipelines.

1. **Setter**: `pod_combat_move_init_effects.txt` —
   `POD_combat_move_set_X` per-flag setter OR use the generic
   `POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = X }`.
2. **Reader**: the gate that consumes the flag — typically
   `pod_combat_resolve_contact_effect`, `pod_combat_check_guard_effect`,
   or another pipeline stage in `pod_combat_effects.txt` /
   `pod_combat_move_effects.txt`. Reads via
   `scope:move.var:X` or `has_variable = X`.
3. **Error suppression**:
   `pod_combat_suppress_optional_move_var = { VAR = X }` in
   `pod_combat_error_suppression_effects.txt`.
4. **Presentation**: if the flag changes what players need to know,
   update the detailed move-card callouts in
   `pod_combat_skill_guis.txt` and their keys in
   `POD_combat_static_l_english.yml`. Audit the independent compact and
   sheet surfaces too: their text dispatchers live in
   `POD_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`, while some icon cells bind directly
   in `pod_combat_skills_window.gui`. Existing `requires_hit_roll`
   demonstrates all three paths.
5. **Authoring**: move blocks call
   `POD_combat_move_set_flag = { NAME = X }` directly. Document the new
   flag in the Combat Move Authoring Guide's presence-flag table.
6. **Documentation**: `COMBAT_MOVE_AUTHORING_GUIDE.info` is the
   designer-facing manual contract. Explain which delivery or behavior
   combinations require the flag.
7. **Capability composition**: no attack-type defaults are applied at
   runtime. Every move that needs the capability must set the flag
   explicitly in its setter block.

#### Adding a marker character modifier

A marker modifier is an effect-free CK3 character modifier that one move
stamps on its user (§12c, setter `POD_combat_move_set_marker_modifier =
{ KEY = ... }`, story var `marker_modifier`) so that a **later** move can
test for it through the `has_modifier` condition predicate. The shipped
example is `pod_combat_anima_flare`: a Terrestrial Exalted flare raises
the marker once per fight, and the Charm moves carry `cond_mode = bonus`
/ `cond_predicate = has_modifier` / `bonus_effect = ap_refund`, so they
partially refund their own AP while it is up. This is how "your next
actions are cheaper" is expressed without an AP-cost-modifier layer.

To add another marker key, in lockstep:

1. **Modifier block** in `common/modifiers/pod_combat_modifiers.txt`,
   plus its name and `_desc` keys in `POD_combat_static_l_english.yml`.
   Keep it a pure marker: a numeric effect would also apply outside the
   fight, since CK3 character modifiers are day-scoped.
2. **Apply case**: a `flag:<key>` arm in the §12c switch in
   `pod_combat_move_effects.txt`. `add_character_modifier` needs a
   literal key, so the switch cannot be parameterised over the flag.
3. **Condition case**: a `flag:<key>` arm in
   `pod_combat_cond_has_modifier_trigger`
   (`pod_combat_condition_dispatch_triggers.txt`).
4. **Name dispatchers**: a case in `PODCombatCondMarkerName` (switches
   on `cond_param`) and in `PODCombatAppliedMarkerName` (switches on
   `marker_modifier`), both in `POD_combat_log_custom_loc.txt`. Both
   resolve to the modifier's own name key, so no marker's flavour gets
   baked into a shared phrasing.
5. **Nothing in the combat log.** `pod_combat_log_seg_marker` is
   key-agnostic - it re-roots `PODCombatAppliedMarkerName` onto the
   `move` scope - so step 5 is what makes the new marker render there.
6. **Removal** at all three lifecycle sites: `pod_combat_duel_end_effect`
   and `pod_combat_host_died_release_effect` (`pod_combat_effects.txt`),
   plus the on-the-way-in self-heal inside
   `pod_combat_init_slot_from_character_effect`'s `is_alive` block
   (`pod_combat_group_effects.txt`), beside the soak-tier clears.
7. **Validator suppression**: `pod_combat_suppress_flag = { FLAG = <key> }`
   in `pod_combat_error_suppression_effects.txt` section 4. The key is
   written parametrically by `POD_combat_move_set_marker_modifier` but
   compared literally at steps 3-5, so until some CSV row actually carries
   the key the game logs `Flag '<key>' is used but is never set`. Drop the
   entry once a shipped move carries the key and the macro expansion
   supplies the set on its own.

**Why removal exists in three places.** CK3 character modifiers are
day-scoped, not round-scoped, and a whole fight resolves inside one game
day, so nothing expires on its own during play. Normal teardown strips
the marker at duel end; the host-death path strips it on the abnormal
teardown; the slot-init clear repairs a combatant who entered a fight
still carrying one. The `days = 30` on the apply call is only a leak
backstop for a fight abandoned without either teardown running.

AI wiring is per-primitive, not per-key, so a new key needs none of it:
`marker_modifier` is already part of the setup tactical-intent
membership (kept in lockstep across `pod_combat_ai_effects.txt`,
`pod_combat_triggers.txt`), and the
AP-strand penalty already exempts a move whose `ap_refund` bonus would
fire. That exemption is a guarded `trigger_if` on
`has_variable = bonus_effect`, because boolean blocks do not
short-circuit and almost no move declares a `bonus_effect` at all.
`marker_modifier` also counts as an independent payload in
`pod_combat_move_has_independent_payload_trigger`, so a marker move is
never rejected by the redundancy gate just because a stance or tier
rider it also carries is already covered.

Limits worth knowing before designing around this:

- **A refund is not a discount.** Both affordability gates read the
  gross `ap_cost`, so a move the actor cannot afford stays unclickable
  no matter how large its refund.
- The generator caps an `ap_refund` `bonus_value` strictly below
  `ap_cost` so the move always costs at least 1 AP net. The engine has
  no clamp; that invariant lives only in the generator.
- `ap_refund` had no live users in the catalogue before this feature,
  so its runtime path is barely exercised in play.
- The marker lands on the **user only**. A `team_ally` marker move is
  not supported: the team_ally utility-admissibility arm admits only
  stance / soak-tier / hit-tier riders.
- A move has one condition slot (gate XOR bonus, one bonus effect), so
  spending it on `has_modifier` / `ap_refund` means that move can carry
  no other gate or bonus.
